 2619| >
 2620| > The reason is structural rather than careless. **A rule is a claim about a CLASS and ages
 2621| > well; an exemplar is a frozen claim about ONE INSTANCE, and instances move** -- code is
 2622| > edited, rows are re-scoped, line numbers drift, and a row can be silently conflated with a
 2623| > second defect. Nothing re-checks an example once it is written down, because it reads as
 2624| > illustration rather than as an assertion.
 2625| >
 2626| > **So: an exemplar in this file is a DATED MEASUREMENT wearing the clothes of an
 2627| > illustration.** When a round leans on one, re-derive it rather than cite it -- and if it has
 2628| > decayed, correct it in place and keep the rule. All three today were caught that way, and in
 2629| > all three the instruction survived contact while the example did not.
 2630| 
 2631| **Round scope (the reopening rule):** a witness makes a site ELIGIBLE, never mandatory. A round
 2632| takes ONE site; a second joins only if it is witnessed, in the SAME device and SAME semantic class
 2633| under the third doctrine arm (so one fix design covers both), and covered by the SAME detector
 2634| with no new oracle. **More witnesses create more candidate ROUNDS, never bigger rounds.**
 2635| 
 2636| ### *** TWO CORRECTIONS TO THIS LADDER, 2026-08-26, from `reprowitness`'s held stage. The
 2637| ### ladder failed its OWN exemplar, and its own checker had already written down the second gap. ***
 2638| 
 2639| **(a) CLAUSE (ii) IS SCOPED TO ACCESS WITNESSES, RUNGS 3�?"4 �?" it was written as rung-2�?"4 and is
 2640| UNSATISFIABLE AT RUNG 2.** Rung 2 admits machine construction for init-time defects and names the
 2641| macppc heap OOB (#23). A construction-time OOB **performs no address decode and returns no device
 2642| value**, so it cannot carry a control of the form "a known value returns through the same decode".
 2643| The ladder's own exemplar could not satisfy the ladder's own clause, and had not been able to
 2644| since the day both were written.
 2645| 
13275| ### `sgirekills` -- and my own brief's premise was the thing that broke
13276| 
13277| `dev_sgi_re.c` carries **21** terminating calls inside `DEVICE_ACCESS` �?" seven times the
13278| gdrom file, and the top of the census list. Besides the six width guards above, its adjacent
13279| arm kills on a plain **read** (`fatal("TODO: read from CRIME_RE_TLB_A"); exit(1);`), which is
13280| a *different* class: direction/offset rather than width.
13281| 
13282| > ### The finding that matters is about the queue, not the device
13283| >
13284| > The `gdromcondlies` brief stated as a premise that those were **the last live
13285| > guest-reachable host kills**. That is false, and the first file sampled from the census
13286| > refuted it.
13287| >
13288| > It was written **as a question** (*"have I confused 'the queue knows of no others' with
13289| > 'there are no others'?"*), which is the only reason it was cheap to catch �?" and it was
13290| > caught before the panel answered.
13291| >
13292| > **A queue-complete claim is not a tree-complete one.** The adjudicating seat had flagged
13293| > exactly this limit about its *own* answer hours earlier �?" *"my no-other-live-host-kills
13294| > claim rests on the queue and this file's grep, not a fresh tree-wide exit census"* �?" and
13295| > the brief inherited the gap anyway. The seat named it; I wrote it down as a premise.
13296| 
13297| **21 is how many live terminating calls sit in that file's `DEVICE_ACCESS` handlers.** It is
13298| *not* a claim that 21 are individually reachable �?" `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:1055` stands as the
13299| proof that such a site can be unreachable and take a hand-derived warrant to establish it.
13300| 
13301| ### `storesidedead` -- a row that tests "the store had NO effect" passes when the store never ran
13302| 
13303| *** THE SEED SENTINEL PROTECTS LOADS.  STORES HAVE NO EXECUTION WITNESS. ***  Named by the
13304| fable5 seat in `#496`'s adjudication pass, as a REFINE on that round's own finding.
13305| 
13306| `#496` added `SEED = 0xdead` so an unexecuted LOAD is distinguishable from a load that
13307| genuinely read zero, and `bit()` returns `SEED-NEVER-RAN` so the sentinel cannot itself be
13308| misread as a set bit. Both are right; both are about the READ side.
13309| 
13310| **A row whose oracle is the CONSEQUENCE OF A STORE has no equivalent.** If the store silently
13311| fails to execute, the device stays in its fresh state �?" **and for these rows the fresh state
13312| IS the want.** Seven of `gdrom_cond_probe.py`'s twenty-two: `R5b`, `R5c`, `R7`, `R7b`, `R10`,
13313| `R3`, `R4`. Six others (`R1`, `R2`, `R5`, `R6`, `R8`, `R9`) fail safe, because their wants are
13314| values only a live store produces.
13315| 
13316| > **Why the controls do not cover it, which is what makes this a class rather than a note.**
13317| > The four control rows catch a WHOLE-RUN-DEAD rig, and they are per-run. The contention
13318| > actually measured on `#496` was **per-session intermittent** �?" the probe forks one gxemul
13319| > per row, so one row's session can die while every other row and all four controls run fine.
13320| > A per-run control cannot see a per-session failure.
13321| 
13322| **NOT a floor breach.** Every enumerated mutant still reds when the apparatus runs, and no run
13323| has exhibited the false pass. It is a latent apparatus risk, not a measured escape, so it
13324| files under the stopping rule rather than reopening `#496`.
13325| 
13326| **The sketch, and it costs nothing for five of the seven:** assert the decline announce �?"
13327| already in the transcript, count == 1 �?" inside the same row's `got` string. That announce is
13328| the store's own execution proof; it cannot appear unless the guest store reached the device
13329| handler. `R10` needs a trailing canary decline instead, since silence *is* its oracle.
13330| 
13331| **`R3`/`R4` are the hard pair and no cheap form is known:** a mid-sequence store death leaves
13332| count == 1, which is exactly the want, so the announce trick is circular there.
13333| 
13334| **Generality, with the sweep deliberately NOT proposed:** any pty-driven probe in this tree
13335| whose oracle is *"the device state did not change"* has the same hole, because not-changing is
13336| what a dead session produces. The population is unmeasured and a sweep would be `#392`'s
13337| shape. What is proposed is the write-time question �?" ***for each row, what does a dead session
13338| score?*** If the answer is the want, the row needs an execution witness.
13339| 
13340| ## 2026-08-30 (later) -- the sgi_re census, and the width class is now WITNESSED
13341| 
13342| ### `widthkillclass` -- RUNG-3 WITNESS, 10/10, and it is the first for this class outside `dev_wdc.c`
13343| 
13344| Built by the agy seat on an unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`. Preserved at
13345| `regress/artifacts/widthkillclass/witness_20260830/`.
13346| 
13347| | row | result |
13348| |---|---|
13349| | `sw` (len=4) to `CRIME_RE_TLB_A` | `exit1` �?" `TODO: unimplemented len=4 for CRIME_RE_TLB_A` |
13350| | `sh` (len=2) | `exit1` �?" len=2 |
13351| | `sb` (len=1) | `exit1` �?" len=1 |
13352| | **`sd` (len=8), the ACCEPT control** | **`exit0` �?" survives** |
13353| 
13354| **That accept row is what makes it a WIDTH defect rather than an unserviceable offset**, which
13355| is a different and already-fixed class. Plus both mandatory controls: liveness through RAM,
13356| and device-signature twice (`CRIME_DE_STATUS` reads its reset `0x1e000000` where RAM reads 0).
13357| 
13358| The guest address `0xb5001000` was **verified against `machine_sgi.c` and the device's own
13359| `memory_device_register`**, not taken from the report.
13360| 
13361| `CLASS_WITNESS`, deliberately ungated: its kill rows invert the day the class is fixed.
13362| 
13363| ### `sgirekills` -- 20 of 21 reachable, and ONE IS PROVABLY DEAD CODE
13364| 
13365| Four classes, which means **at least four candidate rounds, not one**:
13366| 
13367| | class | n | shape |
13368| |---|---:|---|
13369| | WIDTH | 7 | `len != 8` on TLB_A/B/C, TEX, LINEAR_A/B; plus `sgi_de: len = %i` |
13370| | DIRECTION | 6 | `TODO: read from ...` �?" reached by a **legal-width 8-byte READ** |
13371| | BADVAL | 6 | one `sgi_de` drawing op, five `sgi_mte` mode/depth/bytemask arms |
13372| | BADOFFSET | 1 | any access to an unregistered sub-window |
13373| 
13374| > ### The unreachable one must NOT be "fixed", and I verified this by opening the file
13375| >
13376| > `[ sgi_mte: UNIMPLEMENTED read/write len %i ]` sits in the trailing `else` of
13377| > `if (len == 4) { �?� } else if (len != 4) { �?� } else { fatal; exit(1); }`.
13378| >
13379| > For every integer `len` one of the first two arms is taken, so the third **can never
13380| > execute**. A round that de-fatalised it would be editing unreachable code and its detector
13381| > could carry no row �?" the vacuous shape in its purest form. The census is what stops that
13382| > being attempted.
13383| 
13384| **Honest bound:** reachability is derived from address-decode reading, not measured, *except*
13385| for the WIDTH class, which now has the witness above. DIRECTION, BADVAL and BADOFFSET have no
13386| witness, and a round on any of them owes one first.
13387| 
13388| **The open scope question this does NOT settle:** a WIDTH guard and a DIRECTION guard sit in
13389| the *same arm* at the same offset and are still different semantic classes. Whether they
13390| nonetheless share one fix design is exactly what the next research pass should answer rather
13391| than assume.
13392| 
13393| ## 2026-08-30 (later still) -- two findings from farming codex while agy was quota-walled
13394| 
13395| ### `widthkillclass` -- the pcc2 half is HALF what I filed, and the mechanism is verified
13396| 
13397| I filed "dev_pcc2.c 10 sites" with the caveat that its instantiation was not checked and not
13398| claimed. It is now checked: **5 of the 10 width anchors are guest-reachable.**
13399| 
13400| `cpus/cpu_m88k_instr_loadstore.c`, under `#ifndef LS_1` �?" i.e. every **non-byte** access:
13401| 
13402| ```c
13403| 	/*  Check alignment:  */
13404| 	if (addr & (LS_SIZE - 1)) {
13405| 		m88k_exception(cpu, M88K_EXCEPTION_MISALIGNED_ACCESS, 0);
13406| ```
13407| 
13408| **and that precedes `cpu->memory_rw()`.** So a misaligned non-byte access raises a *guest*
13409| exception and never reaches the device handler.
13410| 
