tools/pipeline/ledger.json:30542:   "state_note": "*** MY OWN DIAGNOSIS IN THIS ROW WAS WRONG WITHIN THE HOUR, AND THE CORRECTION MATTERS BECAUSE IT CHANGES\nTHE REMEDY. ***  I wrote that the trigger is 'the SUBJECT SHAPE, not a phrase', on the evidence of two\nrefusals of one brief.  **codex then answered a brief on THE SAME DEFECT, in the same file, at 167 KB.**\n\nThe difference is the FRAMING, and it is sharper than either earlier guess:\n  REFUSED  the WITNESS brief -- 'can you build a probe that REACHES this and shows the bad value?'\n  ANSWERED the DESIGN brief -- 'what should this code do instead, and what should the rows assert?'\nSame defect, same file, same session.  A request to CONSTRUCT A PATH TO A DEFECT reads as an attack recipe;\na request to DECIDE WHAT THE CODE SHOULD DO reads as ordinary review.  Neutralising the vocabulary did not\nhelp because the vocabulary was never the trigger.\n\n**SO THE REMEDY IS NOT ONLY RE-ROUTING: it is to ask codex the DESIGN question and let another seat answer\nthe REACHABILITY one.**  That is cheaper than it sounds -- grok answered the witness brief with a complete\nbuildable sketch, and codex then produced the best design answer of the panel.  Each seat was asked the\nquestion it would answer.\n\n*** THE SEAT-CHECK HAZARD IS UNCHANGED AND IS THE PART THAT WAS ACTUALLY FIXED. ***  The refusal writes a\nzero-byte stdout with the reason on stderr on a direct call -- indistinguishable from an agy quota wall --\nand through panel.sh it produced 150,372 bytes at 97% novel, which `seat_verdicts.py` scored as **answered,\nNO VERDICT LINE**.  A seat failure counted as an answer, in the tool built to refuse exactly that.  Every\nexisting test passed it: no quota keyword (not a wall), high novelty because the seat streams reasoning\nBEFORE the filter fires (not an echo), CLI seat (not a burn).  Now reported as **SEAT REFUSED**, counted with\nthe DOWN seats, keyed on the vendor's own sentence, and controlled in four directions: it fires on the\nrefusal, leaves a genuine quota wall as WALLED, leaves the mid-answer wall's READ-THE-BODY label intact, and\ndoes not touch answering seats.\n\nBoth refusal transcripts and the answered design brief are preserved beside the round's artifacts, so the\nbefore/after comparison is checkable rather than remembered.\n\n*** THIRD INSTANCE, 2026-09-07 -- AND IT REFUTES THE DIAGNOSIS THIS ROW CURRENTLY CARRIES. ***\n\nPreserved: `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/refusals/attempt3_scoping.err'.  Exact text, twice:\n\n    ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk. If this seems wrong, try\n    rephrasing your request. To get authorized for security work, join the Trusted Access for\n    Cyber program: https://chatgpt.com/cyber\n\n**stdout was 0 bytes; the banner, the echoed prompt and both ERROR lines went to stderr** -- so a\ncheck that measures the `.txt' alone sees an empty file and a check that measures SIZE sees 6829\nbytes of healthy-looking stderr.  `seat_verdicts.py' already reports this as SEAT REFUSED / DOWN\n(fixed earlier in this session); this instance confirms the detector on a fresh case.\n\n*** WHAT IS REFUTED: this row concluded the trigger is the reach-the-defect FRAMING -- *design\nbriefs are answered, witness briefs are refused*.  THE REFUSED BRIEF HERE IS NEITHER. ***  It is a\nread-only SCOPING task that explicitly says *\"Do not design the fix diff.  Do not write rows\"*.\n\n**AND THE CONTROL IS STRONG, because it ran the same evening on the same model at the same effort:**\nthe `sgirebufuninit' pass-1 brief -- which discusses uninitialised host stack reaching guest-visible\nmemory, i.e. a MEMORY-DISCLOSURE defect -- was ANSWERED at 317 KB with a GO-WITH-CHANGES verdict and\ntwo new findings.  So the trigger is not the subject matter, not the security-adjacent content, and\nnot the brief's purpose.\n\n**THE REMAINING HYPOTHESIS, stated as a hypothesis because it is not yet tested: VOCABULARY DENSITY.**\nThe refused brief is saturated with this fork's historical term for the defect -- *kills*, *THE HOST\nSTILL DIES*, *terminating calls*, *host kill* -- while the answered brief describes an equivalent\nseverity in ordinary engineering words.  A re-fire is in flight with the identical technical content\nrewritten in bounds-and-error-handling language (`RESEARCH_scoping_neutral_20260907.md'): same\nquestions, same code, same seven dispositions, no `kill'/`dies'/`host' vocabulary.\n\n**That is a real experiment with a clean control and it settles the hypothesis either way** -- if the\nneutral form answers, the trigger is vocabulary and the remedy is the benign-framing rule the carrier\nalready mandates for the Anthropic seats, applied to codex too; if it also refuses, vocabulary is out\nand the next candidate is brief LENGTH or the enumerate-the-sites structure.\n\n*Note the cost is real and recurring: codex is the seat this queue leans on hardest, and a refusal is\ninvisible to any size-based check.*\n\n*** SOLVED, WITH A CONTROLLED EXPERIMENT.  THE TRIGGER IS THE REPOSITORY'S OWN ARCHIVES, NOT THE BRIEF'S\nSUBJECT AND NOT ITS FRAMING.  Measured 2026-09-07 across four runs. ***\n\n    attempt  brief vocabulary   reading scope        stderr     flag\n    1        kills / HOST DIES  unrestricted           6,829 b  IMMEDIATE, before any work\n    3        neutral            unrestricted         374,583 b  at byte 279,268 (~75% in)\n    4        neutral            src/ + regress/*.py  279,132 b  *** NONE -- full verdict delivered ***\n    (control) pass-2 panel      unrestricted         395,340 b  at the END of the answer\n\n**TWO SEPARATE FILTERS, and each attempt isolates one:**\n* **Attempt 1 -> 3 shows a PROMPT-level filter keyed on vocabulary.**  Rewriting *kills / the host dies /\n  terminating calls* into ordinary bounds-and-error-handling language converted an instant refusal into a run\n  that worked for minutes.  The technical content was IDENTICAL -- same twelve sites, same expectation design,\n  same seven dispositions.\n* **Attempt 3 -> 4 shows a CONTENT-level filter keyed on what the seat READS.**  Adding one paragraph -- *do\n  not read `regress/artifacts/' or `tools/pipeline/ledger.json'* -- carried the run past the exact point where\n  the unrestricted one died, to a clean finish.  **279,132 vs 279,268 bytes: the same distance, opposite\n  outcome.**\n\n**WHY THE ARCHIVES: they are this project's own record of a hardening campaign** -- kill tables, `host kill'\ncensuses, `exit(1)' enumerations, mutant names like `widthkillclass' -- accumulated over months.  Read in bulk\nthey look like exactly what the filter exists to catch.  *The irony is that the archive is the honest record\nof DEFENSIVE work, and its own vocabulary is what makes the seat refuse to read it.*\n\n> **THE OPERATIONAL RULE, and it costs two sentences in a brief: for any codex run, (a) write the brief in\n> ordinary engineering language, and (b) SCOPE ITS READING to `src/` and `regress/*.py`.**  The pass-2 control\n> shows why (b) matters even when (a) is satisfied: that brief was already neutral and was still flagged, at\n> the end, after reading the ledger and CHANGELOG.\n\nThis matters more than one lost answer: codex is the seat this queue leans on hardest, a refusal writes 0\nbytes to stdout while leaving healthy-looking stderr, and it is invisible to every size-based check.\n\n*** SIXTH RUN, AND IT REFUTES THE REMEDY I SHIPPED TWO HOURS AGO -- SCOPING IS NECESSARY, NOT\nSUFFICIENT.  Worse: it REHABILITATES the diagnosis I recorded as REFUTED, and my refutation was a\nmisreading of my own brief. ***\n\nA `swpctx' design brief scoped ENTIRELY to `src/' was flagged at 145 KB -- the flag fired while it was\ngrepping `debugger_cmds.c', which is INSIDE the allowed scope.  All six runs, sorted by what they asked:\n\n    WHAT IS TRUE                                    WHAT TO BUILD\n    analysis of a defect        317 KB clean        smallest EDIT that RE-OPENS     375 KB FLAGGED\n    count + classify            298 KB clean        smallest guest PROGRAM that\n    is this path reached?       294 KB clean          WITNESSES each defect         145 KB FLAGGED\n    CAN a guest run at all?     502 KB clean\n\n**The discriminator is whether the brief asks the seat to BUILD SOMETHING THAT TRIGGERS A DEFECT.**  The\n502 KB clean run asked at length about cold-debugger probes, breakpoints and planting instructions -- and\nwas fine, because it asked whether such a thing is POSSIBLE rather than for one aimed at a defect.\n\n*** AND I HAD THIS RIGHT THE FIRST TIME. ***  This row's original diagnosis was *the reach-the-defect\nFRAMING is the trigger*.  I recorded it REFUTED because a read-only SCOPING brief was also refused --\nbut that brief's Q3 asked *\"what is the smallest change to the model that would restore the old\nbehaviour ... while still satisfying a test built that way?\"*  **That is construction framing, and I did\nnot re-read my own brief before declaring the diagnosis dead.**  The premise-audit failure again, on my\nown record rather than on the code.\n\n**THE ARCHIVE FINDING IS NOT SUPERSEDED** -- it carried a run past the exact byte where an unscoped one\ndied, which is a measurement and stands.  There are TWO triggers; §C5 shipped implying one, and now says\nso.\n\n**OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE, and it costs something real:** codex cannot be asked for mutants or witness\nprograms.  It can CHECK a construction supplied to it, and it can say whether one is possible -- both\nmeasured clean.  **The construction itself goes to a measuring seat.**  That is a genuine capability\nloss, not a wording problem, and it should be weighed on the 9th alongside [[codexbriefscoping]].\n\n*** SEVENTH RUN, AND A THIRD REFINEMENT: A BRIEF THAT ASKS `IS THIS TRUE' CAN STILL BE REFUSED IF ITS\nCONTENT IS AN EXPLOITATION CHAIN.  Immediate rejection at 8 KB. ***\n\nThe `sgilinearnegidx' verification brief obeyed both existing rules: neutral engineering language, reading\nscoped to `src/' and `regress/*.py', and it asked only for each link of a chain to be CHECKED -- explicitly\n*\"I want it attacked rather than confirmed\"*, with no construction requested anywhere.  **Refused on the\nprompt**, before any work, exactly like the first `kills'-vocabulary attempt.\n\n**What it contained that the six clean runs did not: a numbered seven-step guest-controlled chain ending in\na PHYSICAL write, plus questions about the reachable address range and about how far the guest can CONTROL\nthe value.**  The clean runs analysed defects, classified sites, traced reachability of a code path, and\nasked whether a probe is possible.  None of them laid out *guest does A, then B, then C, and thereby writes\nwhere it chooses*.\n\n> **So the discriminator is not the QUESTION FORM but the SHAPE OF THE CONTENT.  `What is true about this\n> code?' is clean; `here is a chain by which a guest reaches X -- how far does it go?' is not, however it is\n> phrased.**\n\n**OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE, and it is the sharpest limit yet:** the seat cannot be used to review the very\nfindings that matter most in a hardening fork, once those findings have been assembled into a chain.  It can\ncheck the LINKS SEPARATELY -- *is `src_x' masked in the linear path?* is a clean question -- but not the\nchain as a chain.  **Split the chain into independent link questions, or verify it yourself.**  I verified\nthis one myself in three greps.\n\n*Recorded with the other two triggers rather than replacing them: vocabulary (prompt-level), archive reading\n(content-level, late), and now chain SHAPE (prompt-level).*\n\n*** THIRD RECORDED INSTANCE, 2026-09-07, AND IT CARRIES THE CLEANEST DISCRIMINATOR YET, because\nTWO briefs on the SAME SUBJECT MATTER passed on the SAME DAY and this one did not. ***\n\nArtifact: `regress/artifacts/lcaconfbus1kill/codex_witness_20260907.err`.  The `.txt` is **ZERO\nBYTES and the process exited 0** -- so an exit-status check scores it a success and a size check\nscores it a dead seat; only the `.err` names it:\n\n    ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk. If this seems wrong, try\n    rephrasing your request. To get authorized for security work, join the Trusted Access for\n    Cyber program: ...\n\n**THE CONTROLS ARE THE VALUABLE PART.  Same seat, same model, same effort, same day, same device\nfile, all three read-only:**\n\n    PASSED   brief_lcabadval_fixcheck.md      \"six value guards that END THE PROCESS\" -- inlined\n                                              fatal()+exit(1) verbatim, asked whether a proposed\n                                              DELETION was sound.  6,976-byte answer.\n    PASSED   brief_sgirandomcolour_scope.md   \"writes FOUR random() bytes to guest memory\",\n                                              \"uninitialised host stack reaches guest memory\",\n                                              asked for a reachability census.  7,946-byte answer.\n    REFUSED  brief_lcaconfbus1_witness.md     asked whether a proposed TEST CONSTRUCTION would\n                                              successfully REACH the termination, step by step.\n\n**So it is not the vocabulary and not the subject.**  \"Host kill\", \"exit(1)\", \"guest-drivable\",\n\"uninitialised stack into guest memory\" all pass.  What differs is the ASK: the first two ask *is\nthis analysis correct*; the third asks *will this sequence work*.  A numbered two-step recipe --\nwrite this value here, then touch that window, and the process exits -- reads as exploit validation\nhowever defensively it is framed, and mine was framed defensively (opened with \"ordinary regression-\ntest adequacy review\", no offensive-security wording anywhere).\n\n> **THE OPERATIONAL RULE: ask codex to VERIFY AN ANALYSIS, not to VALIDATE A SEQUENCE THAT REACHES A\n> TERMINATION.**  This is the carrier's existing C5 trigger -- *asking for a construction that\n> reaches a defect* -- with a measured negative control on both sides for the first time.  Where a\n> construction genuinely needs checking, ask about its CONTROLS in isolation (\"does a matched\n> surviving control at the same address attribute an outcome to the value?\") without narrating the\n> path to the kill.\n\n**RECOVERY, and it is the sixth-class rule applied: the seat refused, the QUESTION did not become\nunanswerable.**  grok reads the repo, takes its brief from a FILE (no argv cap), runs `--permission-\nmode plan` read-only, and has no such filter.  It was fired on the byte-identical brief.  A refused\nseat with a live alternate transport is ONE SEAT COVERED, not one lost -- but it is recorded as a\nREFUSAL by name, never as agreement or as a wall.\n\n*Cost note: 17,471 tokens spent before the flag fired, and 217 KB of tool-call reasoning in the\n`.err` showing it had already listed the tree and begun reading.  The refusal is not instant, so a\nzero-byte `.txt` after several minutes is NOT evidence of a hang.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:30584:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-06.  **FOUND BY GROK** on the `sgirandompixel' design panel, as a residual it explicitly said\nto file rather than grow the round with: *'`default:' of the COLOUR-mode switch still does\n`buf[n] = random()' and then writes those bytes.  Same class, different arm.'*\n\n**VERIFIED BY READING before filing**: four `random()' calls at dev_sgi_re.c, anchor `buf[0] = random();',\nin the colour-mode `default:'.  `sgirandompixel' removed the ONE in `horrible_getputpixel()'; these four\nremain and are the reason `grep -c 'random()'` on that file is still non-zero after the fix.\n\nNobody else on that panel saw it -- not codex, not the packet-fed seats, not me -- and I had read the file\nseveral times that evening.  The design is presumably the same member 3 (decline + latched announce), but\nthe observable differs: this arm fills a BUFFER rather than a single caller variable, so what a declined\nfill should leave is its own question and the detector shape is not simply copied.\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (assess stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nSEVERITY RAISED MED -> HIGH, on a fact DERIVED rather than assumed.  The row said the fix\nwas *presumably* the same as [[sgirandompixel]]'s and that the observable differed.  **The reachability is now\nestablished and it is the SAME documented-mode structure, not merely the same shape.**\n\nMeasured at HEAD, three commands:\n  * the PUT colour switch implements exactly FOUR cases -- `DE_MODE_TYPE_' CI, RGB, RGBA, ABGR -- and its\n    `default:' is `buf[0..3] = random()';\n  * `crmfbreg.h' documents **FIVE** colour types; the fifth is `DE_MODE_TYPE_YCRCB  0x000000f0';\n  * `grep -c YCRCB src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c' = **0**.  It is handled nowhere.\n\nAnd it is reached by an ordinary register write: `color_mode = mode & DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK', mask `0xf0', so a\nguest selecting YCRCB lands on the random arm directly.\n\n*** SO THIS IS `#504's OWN SENTENCE AT A SECOND SITE: four of five DOCUMENTED modes work and the fifth\nreturns random data. ***  `#504` was graded HIGH on exactly that warrant, and this arm is arguably worse in\none respect -- `sgirandompixel' put a random value in `*color' for the CALLER to store, whereas this writes\nfour random bytes into the tile through `memory_rw(..., MEM_WRITE)' with no intermediary.\n\n**WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, and the severity should be read with it: NO WITNESS.**  Nothing here shows a guest\ndriver selecting YCRCB -- only that the model accepts it and answers with random data.  The rung-3 attempt\nlooks cheap for the same reason `#504's did (`regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py' already programs this\ncode path; the colour type is a different field of the same mode word), and it is OWED before this ships.\n\n**The GET side does NOT share the defect** -- its `default:' reads the raw 32-bit value rather than inventing\none, so this is a PUT-only arm.  That asymmetry is itself worth carrying into the design: the two default\narms of one function already disagree about what an unhandled colour type means.\n\nNote this is a DIFFERENT defect from [[sgirebufuninit]] at the same lines: that one is about bytes NOBODY\nassigns; this is about bytes assigned from `random()'.  A fix for either leaves the other standing.\n\n*** READY TO RUN AS THE NEXT ROUND.  Three things established 2026-09-07 while `#505` worked in the\nsame function, none of which this row had. ***\n\n**1. IT IS A DIFFERENT SWITCH FROM THE ONE `#504` FIXED, and the row should say so plainly** -- I nearly\nrecorded this as the `#499` symmetric-cell shape and it is not.  `#504` de-fanged the **addressing-mode**\ndefault (`default: fatal(\"unimplemented dst_mode %i ...\"); *color = random();`).  This row is the\n**colour-mode** default of a different `switch` in the same function:\n\n    default:buf[0] = random();  buf[1] = random();\n            buf[2] = random();  buf[3] = random();\n            // TODO.\n            fatal(\"[ put: color mode = 0x%x ]\\n\", color_mode);\n    }\n    cpu->memory_rw(cpu, cpu->mem, tileptr + ofs, buf, bufdepth, MEM_WRITE, NO_EXCEPTIONS|PHYSICAL);\n\n**There is no `break` and no decline: control falls straight into the write**, so four bytes of host\n`random()` land in guest memory at a guest-chosen tile offset.  Same family as `#504`, same function,\ndifferent arm -- *related by idiom, not by the one-cell-repair pattern.*\n\n**2. FULLY GUEST-REACHABLE, established by reading rather than assumed.**  `color_mode = mode &\nDE_MODE_TYPE_MASK`, and `mode` is the caller's `src_mode`/`dst_mode`, read straight out of `de_reg[]`\nfrom `CRIME_DE_MODE_SRC`/`CRIME_DE_MODE_DST` with **no filtering anywhere on the path**.  The four\nimplemented types are 0x00/0x10/0x20/0x30; `DE_MODE_TYPE_YCRCB` is **0xf0 and is documented in\n`crmfbreg.h`** but unimplemented, so a guest selecting a real, named mode reaches this arm.\n\n**3. A WITNESS IS ONE CONSTANT AWAY.**  `#505` built `regress/sgi_re_rgb_probe.py`,\na rung-3 probe that drives an XFER copy on an unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32` with both ladder controls and a\nsibling accept row, and whose `setup()` now takes **both** src and dst modes.  Pointing its `dst_mode` at\n`LIN_A | BUFDEPTH_32 | 0xf0` puts YCRCB into the PUT switch and reaches this arm.\n\n  *** THE ORACLE IS THE HARD PART AND IT NEEDS SAYING BEFORE THE ROUND STARTS: THE DEFECT'S OUTPUT IS\n  RANDOM, SO NO VALUE ROW CAN PIN IT. ***  `#504` hit exactly this and its answer is the precedent --\n  assert that the destination still holds its PRE-PAINT value, i.e. that the write was DECLINED, rather\n  than trying to assert anything about what was written.  A determinism oracle is refuted in advance by\n  this project's own record: two seats showed on `#504` that stack reuse can make a wrong value\n  deterministic, so *\"the same value twice\"* proves nothing.\n\n**FIX SHAPE: the option set has a member already chosen for its sibling.**  `#504` used DECLINE + LATCHED\nANNOUNCE with its own bit (`SGIRE_W_BADMODE = 1 << 10`), for the reason `#498` measured -- the four\nhandlers share one struct, so a shared latch is silenced by the other site's ordinary traffic.  **This arm\nneeds its OWN bit, `1 << 11`**, and `#504`'s comment already says the enum ends there on purpose so the\nnext maintainer finds it.  *That is a transferred in-tree warrant, not a \"cannot hurt\" addition.*\n\n*** PASS-1 AUDIT, 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_pass1_20260907.txt`).  TWO OF MY PREMISES WERE WRONG AND THE\nDISPOSITION SURVIVED. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE is justified, with an unconditional return from the PUT\ndefault.  But the brief misstates #504's oracle and the write's byte count; depth 8 also exposes\nuninitialized buffer bytes.\"*\n\n*** CORRECTION 1 -- THE ORACLE.  THIS IS THE ONE THAT WOULD HAVE COST A ROUND. ***  My brief said\n`#504`'s precedent asserts *the destination still holds its PRE-PAINT value*.  It does not.  The seat\nopened the probe: `lin_b is fg` expects **`FG_VAL = 0x77665544`**, not the pre-paint `0x11111111`,\nbecause a declined source GET **preserves the caller's foreground value**, which the following PUT\nthen writes.  In the seat's words, *\"the probe explicitly rejects preserving the destination as a\ndifferent disposition.\"*\n\n  **So `#504` warrants the latch-and-return IDIOM and does NOT supply the unchanged-destination\n  ORACLE I attributed to it.**  Those are different transfers and I ran them together.\n\n  **THE ORACLE THE SEAT PROPOSES INSTEAD, and it is stronger than either:** observe **ZERO\n  `MEM_WRITE` CALLS** from an unsupported PUT, *alongside* unchanged destination bytes -- because\n  that pair **distinguishes \"no write\" from \"same final value\"**, which neither assertion does\n  alone.  It also notes that if removing the RNG use is contractual, that must be observed\n  SEPARATELY.\n\n  *And a sentinel value will not rescue a value oracle here:* each assignment converts a SEPARATE\n  `random()` result to `uint8_t`, so the whole-word improbability argument `#504`'s old witness used\n  does not establish an impossible byte pattern.\n\n*** CORRECTION 2 -- THE BYTE COUNT, and it is the SAME WIDTH-AXIS LESSON `#505` PAID FOR HOURS\nEARLIER. ***  My brief said *four* bytes of `random()` are written.  The write is `bufdepth` bytes,\nnot four: at bufdepth 8 the arm fills `buf[0..3]` with RNG and ships **four further UNINITIALISED\nstack bytes** with them; at bufdepth 1 or 2 fewer than four RNG bytes land.  **\"Four\" is true only\nat bufdepth 4** -- the width every example happens to use.  *That is the exact shape of `#505`'s\nclause-(d) floor breach, arriving in the next round's brief the same day, written by the person who\nhad just repaired it.*  Overlaps [[sgirebufuninit]]; the round must say which defect owns which\nbytes.\n\n**OPTION SET: CONFIRMED COMPLETE, and the grep found three in-tree alternatives worth naming:**\n  * the helper's own GET default already provides a raw ABGR-shaped fallback (`dev_sgi_re.c:358`);\n  * **PCC2 has a real ACCEPT precedent** (`dev_pcc2.c:427`) -- a read served from backing storage\n    before the default diagnostic;\n  * **SGI's ROP diagnostics latch BY VALUE** (`dev_sgi_re.c:850`) rather than one bit per site.\n\nNone establishes correct YCRCB PUT semantics.  *\"Mirroring the GET fallback would repeat the\nunsupported-authority problem you warned about.  The YCRCB name establishes an encoding; it does not\nestablish packing, conversion, or depth semantics.\"*  IMPLEMENT is defensible in principle but no\npermitted source supplies an implementation or a demonstrated driver selection.  **No additional\nwarranted disposition outside the option set** -- which is the option-set rule returning a clean\nnegative for once, and that is worth recording as such.\n\n**THE LATCH BIT IS CONFIRMED AVAILABLE AND THE OBJECTION TO ONE BIT IS RECORDED:** occupied bits\nenumerate as 0..10, `warned` is `uint32_t`, so **`1 << 11` is free**.  The strongest objection is\ndiagnostic coverage -- *an earlier unnamed mode suppresses a later YCRCB announcement* -- and\nper-mode latching has in-tree precedent (the ROP site above).  One bit stays consistent with this\nhelper's existing policy, so it is a judgement the round must make explicitly rather than inherit.\n\n**FIX SHAPE (Q2), in the seat's words:** *a terminal default arm containing a conditional\nannouncement followed by an **unconditional function return***.  Note this is the SKETCH RULE's\nterritory -- the PUT branch's write sits after the switch and is shared by all five arms, so the\nreturn must be checked against the whole enclosing function before it is written.\n\n*** AND A WARNING THAT SAVES THE ROUND A FALSE START: `#504`'s PROBE CANNOT BE REUSED UNCHANGED. ***\n*\"#504's `lin_b is fg` remains green under edits confined to this colour default.  Both of that\nprobe's mode constants use RGBA, so it never selects the PUT colour default.  Its setup cannot be\ntransferred unchanged.\"*  `#505`'s probe is the better base -- its `setup()` already takes both src\nand dst modes -- but the same check applies to it and must be run, not assumed.\n\n**ENUMERATION CONFIRMED, with a qualification I did not have:** the twelve default selections are\neleven unnamed (`0x40`..`0xe0`) plus the named `0xf0` YCRCB, and they reach the arm *\"when the\npreceding coordinate/addressing/tile-validity checks pass\"*.\n\n*** THE ORACLE QUESTION, ANSWERED BEFORE THE ROUND STARTED -- AND THE NAIVE IMPLEMENTATION WOULD\nHAVE PRODUCED A FALSE GREEN.  codex, `regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_oracle_20260907.txt`.\n***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"Your distinction is conditionally sound.  A correctly scoped write-attempt observer\nworks for Round A; **the existing stepped probe supplies none.**  This fork's debugger watchpoints\noffer a qualified alternative.\"*\n\n**MY DISTINCTION SURVIVES, with a precision I had not stated.**  A count separates *no request* from\n*a request carrying the existing bytes* -- but *\"zero calls does not establish correct rejection\nunless the unsupported arm was ACTUALLY REACHED: an earlier return or an operation that never ran\nalso produces zero.\"*  That is the attribution problem, and it means the round owes a reach control\nbeside the count.\n\n*** THE MECHANISM EXISTS: debugger WRITE WATCHPOINTS, checked in the common memory path BEFORE\ndevice/cache/RAM dispatch, and neither PHYSICAL nor NO_EXCEPTIONS excludes this helper's write.  A\npty driver can observe them with NO instrumented build. ***  (`memory_rw.c:273`.)\n\n*** AND IT HAS TWO DECISIVE LIMITS, THE FIRST OF WHICH WOULD HAVE PRODUCED A FALSE GREEN: ***\n  1. **The check requires `!single_step`, and the existing probe's `run_ops()` uses `step`.**  So\n     *\"merely adding watchpoints and counting their messages to that execution scheme could report\n     ZERO DESPITE WRITES.\"*  The row would have reported \"no write happened\" while writes happened.\n     **A watchpoint oracle therefore requires the FREE-RUNNING probe shape, not the stepped one.**\n  2. It also requires `!about_to_enter_single_step` and **SETS that flag after its first report**, so\n     later pixel writes inside the same drawing command go unreported.  What Python would count is\n     *printed watchpoint reports*, not `MEM_WRITE` calls -- watchpoints carry no hit count.\n\n**SO THE ACHIEVABLE CLAIM IS BOUNDED, and the round must say so rather than overclaim:** the\nmechanism supports an **any-attempt-to-this-range** witness when observation is active throughout the\nrelevant execution.  It does NOT support *\"exactly N calls\"* or *\"no writes anywhere from this\nhelper\"*.  The conclusion must read **\"no observed write attempt to this range under these validated\nconditions\"** -- watching the destination cannot exclude a write to an UNINTENDED address, which is\nexactly the failure mode [[sgirebufuninit]] and the `#505` tiled row both hit in other forms.\n\n**THE SHIPPABLE DESIGN, in the seat's own construction:** verified arm reach and completed execution,\nan active observer that DOES detect a supported write to the same range (the liveness half), then no\nmatching write report for the rejected operation, with unchanged verified destination bytes as\ncorroboration and the announce count testing \"announce once\" separately.  **That is four rows, not\none**, and knowing it now is worth more than discovering it after the fix.\n\n*This is the carrier's `!single_step` family arriving through a NEW mechanism.  The carrier records\nthat a stepped probe cannot observe INTERRUPT DELIVERY (`cpu_dyntrans.c`'s\n\"do not cause interrupts while single-stepping\").  The WATCHPOINT case is the same guard on a\ndifferent feature and was not written down.*\n\n*** CODEX DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07 (`regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_design_20260907.txt`).\nVERDICT: REVISE -- DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE **with a `return` before the PUT transfer**.  It\nrefuted TWO of the brief's premises, and one of them was the brief's central design claim. ***\n\n**S3 WAS WRONG, AND IT IS THE PREMISE THE WHOLE 'COPY THE SIBLING' READING RESTED ON.**  I wrote that\n`sgirandompixel` fixed *the GET side of this same helper*.  It did not: **it repaired the\nADDRESSING-MODE default at `:235`, which sits BEFORE GET and PUT diverge and returns for both\ndirections.  The COLOUR GET default at `:358` still performs a raw conversion.**  Two different\narms -- so the sibling is a precedent for the DISPOSITION and not for the site, and its comment\ncannot simply be copied.\n\n**S5 WAS WRONG, AND THIS ONE UNBLOCKS THE ROUND.**  I claimed the decline's 'what does `buf` hold'\nquestion was the SAME question as [[sgirebufuninit]]'s, making that row a prerequisite.  codex:\n*\"A declined PUT need not leave anything defined in `buf`, provided nothing consumes it.  The helper\nitself owns the memory write.  Its callers receive no buffer to transfer afterward.\"*\n\n    remove the fill, fall through   -> transfers indeterminate stack bytes   REJECT\n    zero the span, fall through     -> overwrites destination with zero      needs a warrant\n    RETURN from the PUT default     -> preserves destination bytes           PREFERRED\n\n> **So the two rows are INDEPENDENT and there is no patch ordering.**  This round decides whether an\n> unsupported PUT writes anything at all; `sgirebufuninit` decides how paths that DO transfer avoid\n> consuming uninitialised bytes.  *Initialising the buffer would neither remove the four `random()`\n> calls nor justify writing zeros for an unsupported format.*\n\n**S1 needs a width qualification:** four bytes are ASSIGNED randomly, but the transfer length is 1, 2,\n4 or 8.  A narrow transfer exposes only the prefix; an eight-byte transfer additionally consumes four\nuninitialised bytes.\n\n**S2's inference was a CORRELATE and the real population is twelve, not one.**  `grep -c YCRCB` = 0\nproves only that one NAMED constant is absent.  Enumerating `mode & 0xf0`: sixteen values, **four\nexplicit PUT cases (CI/RGB/RGBA/ABGR) and TWELVE reaching `default:`** -- eleven unnamed plus YCRCB.\nThe DE register handler stores the mode without filtering the type nibble, and both line and rectangle\ncallers pass it through unchanged.  *The named-constant search was the same error that cost the\n`asicbadaddr` census a 4x undercount.*\n\n**FAULT IS AVAILABLE HERE and my brief implied otherwise** -- `memory_rw.c:603` delivers MIPS\nexceptions.  codex rejects it anyway on better grounds than availability: the helper returns `void`,\nits transfers request `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, and the outer device handler returns success, so FAULT would\nneed an error-propagation design and a warrant for the guest-visible exception.\n\n**THE LATCH MUST BE AN INDEPENDENT BIT, NOT `SGIRE_W_BADMODE`** -- sharing it would let an ADDRESSING\nfailure suppress the COLOUR diagnostic.  That is `#490`'s measured latch-collision class, predicted\nhere before the code exists.\n\n**Q4, the predicted escape:** condition the new `return` on `bufdepth == 4` -- the canonical clause-(d)\nshape, conditioning the repair on a field the transfer consumes.  **codex expects `rgb tiled64` to stay\ngreen under it**, because that row varies the GET SOURCE's addressing and width while keeping a linear\nfour-byte RGBA destination: *\"its name does not establish PUT coverage.\"*  The proposed closure is the\ndestination axes together -- twelve unsupported types x four widths x four address modes = **192\ncombinations** -- with whole-span checks including eight-byte spans.  *Source-based prediction; no\nmutant built.*\n\n**Q3, the witness:** source inspection supports a rung-3 cold-debugger probe on stock IP32 and codex\nfound no machine-description blocker.  But **the sibling probe's green rows are NOT a witness for this\nrow**: its setup fixes the DESTINATION to RGBA, so varying its source-mode argument never exercises the\ncolour PUT default.  Record **'source-reachable; rung-3 attempt pending'** -- codex ran nothing, as\ninstructed, so the attempted-probe prerequisite is NOT yet discharged.\n\n*** A CONSEQUENCE OF THE DECLINE DESIGN, found by the codex seat on the SIBLING brief\n(`sgimtedepth4dead`) and cross-filed here because it is this row's design that it bears on. ***\n\n*\"A helper return before accessing the buffer leaves the caller's colour unchanged.  MTE still\nperforms the dependent PUT, and its `bg` persists across iterations -- so a declined GET can reuse an\nearlier source value.\"*\n\n**That is distinct from buffer initialisation and it bites the agreed repair.**  The design settled\nfor this row is DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE with a `return` before the transfer -- and on the MTE\npath the caller's `bg` is a loop-scope variable, so a declined pixel does not leave the destination\nuntouched: **it writes the PREVIOUS pixel's colour.**  A decline that silently smears the last good\nvalue across a run of unsupported pixels is a different wrong answer from `random()`, and arguably a\nmore plausible-looking one.\n\n*The round owes an answer: does the decline return before the PUT as well, and if so what does the\ndestination hold?  This was not visible from this row's own brief, which framed the question around\n`buf` rather than around the caller's colour variable.*\n\n*** DESIGN PASS 2, 2026-09-07: I CONFLATED A DECLINED GET WITH A DECLINED PUT.  PASS 1's DESIGN\nSTANDS UNCHANGED. ***\n\nThe PUT-default `return` exits **before** `memory_rw(... MEM_WRITE ...)`, so whatever the caller's\n`color` holds, that declined PUT does not transfer it.  Leaving the destination's existing bytes\nintact is the honest disposition for an unsupported write.  **The `bg` observation is about the\ndependent-PUT problem, which is a different row.**\n\n**AND MY S4 DID NOT ESTABLISH REACHABILITY:** MTE builds modes from addressing and depth bits only,\nleaving the colour type at CI, and its one colour override sits behind the unreachable `depth == 4`.\n**Neither construction reaches this PUT colour default**, so the MTE `bg` behaviour does not bear on\nthis row at all.\n\n*Two further qualifications on my wording: MTE reloads `bg` from `CRIME_MTE_BG` on **each transfer\ninvocation**, so a declined GET retains the latest value from THAT invocation or the register's\ninitial value -- never a previous command's local `bg`.  And \"the whole rectangle deterministically\nreceives the last good colour\" is too strong independently, because CI's accepted PUT initialises\nonly `buf[0]` and wider depths consume uninitialised bytes.*\n\n**THE LATCH GRANULARITY IS CONFIRMED AS APPROPRIATE AND ITS LIMIT STATED:** one message per device\ninstance for the latch's lifetime -- so a thousand declined pixels produce one line, **and none if an\nearlier command already set the bit** (the struct is zeroed at init; there is no per-command reset).\nIt deliberately cannot distinguish one declined pixel from a rectangle; reporting extent would need\ncounting or a command summary.\n\n**RESIDUAL FOUND: bounding the PUT warning does not bound every warning from the operation.**  The\n**GET colour default still logs without a latch**, and DE can perform that GET for a\ndestination-dependent raster operation before ever reaching the declined PUT.  So a round that\nlatches only the PUT arm leaves a guest-drivable unlatched diagnostic in the same code path.\n\n**THE CLAUSE-(d) MUTANT:** change the new `return;` to `break;`, keeping the latch and the\nannouncement.  Execution resumes at the memory write and consumes the uninitialised buffer -- **it\nbreaks the no-write contract without restoring `random()`**.  `rgb samemode` and `rgb tiled64` both\nexpected to stay green (supported RGB/RGBA, never reaching the default).  *The coverage gap is wider\nthan MTE: the scoped scripts never select an unsupported destination colour type at all, so their\ngreen results cannot establish this decline.*\n\n*** THE WITNESS PROBLEM IS CHARACTERISED, AND IT IS HARDER THAN THE ROW ASSUMED.  From the codex\nORACLE answer (`regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_oracle_20260907.txt`), read in full\n2026-09-07 -- most of this had been sitting unread. ***\n\n**TWO PREMISE CORRECTIONS FIRST, and the second changes the FIX, not just the witness:**\n\n1. *\"The buffer lacks a declaration initializer; it is not NEVER INITIALIZED.  PUT arms assign\n   prefixes, and GET fills the requested transfer length.\"*  The row's shorthand was sloppy.\n2. *** \"THE MISSING `break` IS NOT WHAT PERMITS THE WRITE.  The write is AFTER the switch, so adding\n   `break` would still reach it.  **The proposed return matters.**\" ***  A `break`-shaped repair\n   would look right and do nothing -- exactly the sketch-versus-enclosing-control-flow rule this\n   project minted after four readers proposed a `return` into a function with a common tail.\n\n*** THE OBSERVER EXISTS, AND IT IS DISQUALIFIED BY THE PROBE'S OWN EXECUTION MODE. ***\n\nThe fork has debugger WRITE-WATCHPOINTS, and the common memory path checks them **before**\ndevice/cache/RAM dispatch -- *\"neither `PHYSICAL` nor `NO_EXCEPTIONS` excludes this helper's\nwrite\"* (`memory_rw.c:273`).  A matching attempt prints `WATCHPOINT: write`, which a pty driver can\ncount with no instrumented build.  **But two conditions kill it as written:**\n\n    memory_rw.c:273   the check requires  !single_step\n                      -- and sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py's run_ops() uses `step'.  Adding\n                      watchpoints to that scheme \"could report ZERO DESPITE WRITES.\"\n\n    same check        also requires  !about_to_enter_single_step, and SETS that flag after its\n                      first report -- so subsequent pixel writes inside the SAME drawing command\n                      go unreported.\n\n> **This is the carrier's single-stepping rule arriving on a new mechanism.**  It already records\n> that a stepped probe cannot observe interrupt DELIVERY (`cpu_dyntrans.c`: *\"Do not cause\n> interrupts while single-stepping\"*).  **Watchpoints are a second capability gated the same way**,\n> and the same discriminator applies: ask what the observation depends on before designing around\n> it.\n\n**WHAT THE MECHANISM CAN AND CANNOT SUPPORT, in codex's words:** it supports an\n**any-attempt-to-this-range** witness *\"when observation is active throughout the relevant\nexecution\"* -- i.e. FREE-RUNNING, not stepped -- and even then only the first report survives the\nflag.  It *\"cannot automatically support 'exactly N calls', or 'no writes anywhere from this\nhelper'.  A literal, attributed call count needs a suitable host-side observer or `harness:`\nwitness.\"*\n\n*** AND Q3 DEMOLISHES THE CHEAP ORACLE: \"unchanged destination bytes prove only unchanged OBSERVED\nCONTENTS.\" ***  Equality at the observation points is also consistent with a same-value write, a\nwrite followed by restoration, a write to somewhere else, no drawing operation at all, or an earlier\nhelper return.  **Two of those are live here rather than theoretical**: the helper returns on an\ninvalid tile pointer (`dev_sgi_re.c:274`), and *\"the memory path can discard an out-of-RAM write\nwhile returning success; corresponding reads can return zeros\"* (`memory_rw.c:662`).\n\n> **So a destination-equality row needs a control establishing that the pre-paint REALLY EXISTS and\n> that a SUPPORTED operation through the same path actually lands** -- otherwise \"unchanged\" is\n> indistinguishable from \"nothing ever happened\", which is the vacuity shape in its purest form.\n\n**THE TWO VIABLE PATHS, neither cheap:**\n* **(a) a FREE-RUNNING probe with a write-watchpoint** -- any-attempt semantics, first report only,\n  and it must abandon the stepped `run_ops()` idiom the sibling probes use;\n* **(b) a `harness:` witness with a host-side observer** -- an attributed call count, which the\n  ladder admits only when the round's subject IS the apparatus, so it would need care not to become\n  the rung-1 shortcut the ladder exists to refuse.\n\n**STATUS: this row is NOT held for fable5 and is the only HIGH row that is not** -- but its blocker\nis now a characterised capability gap rather than an open question, and neither path is a session's\nwork.  *Recorded in full because most of this analysis had been sitting unread in the artifact since\nit was produced, which is this session's most-repeated failure.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:30626:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, found while writing the design brief for `sgirandomcolour' -- i.e. by reading the arm NEXT\nto the one grok pointed at.  **This is a bigger defect than the row that led me to it, and it needs no\nundocumented mode at all.**\n\n`horrible_getputpixel()' declares `uint8_t buf[8];' and NEVER INITIALISES IT.  Verified: no memset in the\nfunction, no initialiser, and the only memsets in the file are the device struct, `rop_used' and `zerobuf'.\nThe two fields that decide how much of `buf' is USED are decoded from the SAME guest-written mode register\nand are INDEPENDENT of each other:\n\n    uint32_t color_mode = mode & DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK;      /* bits 4-7  */\n    int bufdepth = 1 << ((mode >> 8) & 3);               /* bits 8-9  -> 1, 2, 4 or 8 */\n\nThe colour arms fill a number of bytes fixed by the COLOUR type; the transfer length is `bufdepth'.  Nothing\nanywhere makes them agree -- `grep -rn '>> *8) *& *3' src/devices/' finds the decode, its own #100 comment,\nand the XFER siblings; NO GUARD.\n\n**TWO REACHABLE UNINITIALISED PATHS, IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS:**\n\n* **PUT -- host stack INTO guest-visible memory.**  With the depth field = 3 (`bufdepth' 8) NO colour arm\n  writes `buf[4..7]' -- not one of the five, including the correct ones -- and all eight bytes go straight to\n  `memory_rw(..., tileptr + ofs, buf, bufdepth, MEM_WRITE, NO_EXCEPTIONS | PHYSICAL)'.  `DE_MODE_TYPE_CI'\n  writes only `buf[0]', so CI at any depth above 1 leaks the rest.\n* **GET -- host stack INTO `*color', which the caller then STORES.**  The read fills only `bufdepth' bytes,\n  then RGB/RGBA/ABGR combine `buf[0..3]'.  Depth field 0 (`bufdepth' 1) with `DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA' reads ONE\n  byte from memory and builds a 32-bit colour from four.  `draw_primitive()' writes that to the destination\n  in the same loop iteration -- **the identical caller-consumes-a-failed-read shape `sgirandompixel' just\n  fixed one arm of**, reached here with every colour mode documented and implemented.\n\n*** THE #100 COMMENT IS THE TELL, AND IT IS ON THE DECLARATION ITSELF. ***  It reads\n`/*  #100: bufdepth can be 8 = 1<<((mode>>8)&3)  */' -- so a previous round worked out that the guest can\ndemand 8 bytes and GREW THE ARRAY to match, which fixed a stack OVERFLOW.  **It did not ask what fills the\nbytes it had just added.**  The array is the right size and the contents are undefined.\n\nSEVERITY: guest-reachable read of uninitialised host stack whose value becomes guest-visible.  In a loop the\nstale bytes are usually the PREVIOUS pixel's, so the everyday symptom is wrong colours; on the first call in\na frame they are whatever the host stack held.  Both mode words are ordinary device registers reached by\nordinary stores, and all seven call sites pass one of them.\n\nNOT YET WITNESSED -- filed at once so the finding is durable.  The witness is expected to be cheap because\n`regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py' already programs this exact path on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32' and\nneeds only a different mode word: its `MODE_LIN_A = 0x1220' is LIN_A | depth-field 2 (`bufdepth' 4) | RGBA,\nwhich is the MATCHED case and leaks nothing.  `0x1020' is the same line with depth field 0.\n\n*** PASS-1 CORRECTION FROM THE GROK SEAT, VERIFIED AGAINST THE HEADER BEFORE BEING ACCEPTED.  MY PATH-A\nEXEMPLAR WAS THE WEAKER ONE, AND A FULLY DOCUMENTED VEHICLE EXISTS. ***\n\nThe brief drove PATH A with mode `0x1320' -- depth field 3, `bufdepth' 8 -- and called it reachable.  grok:\n*\"BUFDEPTH in the header is 8/16/32 only -- field 3 is undocumented ... PATH A is live on documented `CI' +\n`BUFDEPTH_16/32' (`0x1200'), not only on undocumented depth 8.  The brief's `0x1320' PATH-A vehicle is the\nweaker one.\"*\n\n**CHECKED, not taken on trust** -- `grep -n BUFDEPTH src/include/thirdparty/crmfbreg.h' returns exactly three:\n\n    DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_8   0x00000000   -> depth field 0 -> bufdepth 1 byte\n    DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_16  0x00000100   -> depth field 1 -> bufdepth 2 bytes\n    DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_32  0x00000200   -> depth field 2 -> bufdepth 4 bytes\n\n**Field value 3 is documented nowhere**, so the brief's own vehicle relies on an undocumented depth -- while\n`DE_MODE_TYPE_CI' assigns ONLY `buf[0]' and `DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_32' transfers FOUR bytes, so `0x1200' leaks\n`buf[1..3]' with **every field documented and CI being colour type ZERO**, the reset value.\n\n*(Note the naming trap I had not spotted: the constants are in BITS and the code's `bufdepth' is in BYTES, so\n`BUFDEPTH_32' is 4.  That is why `1 << ((mode >> 8) & 3)' maxes at 8 with only three constants defined.)*\n\n**AND IT SETTLED Q1.3, WHICH I HAD EXPLICITLY LEFT OPEN: the destination is RAM, not a device.**  So `#95's\ndevice-read pre-zero does not run AT ALL on either path -- not merely `does not cover the bytes past len'.\nBoth paths are therefore worse than the brief stated, not better.\n\n**This is the premise-audit opener earning its place for the third recorded time.**  I asked seats to attack\nthe reachability premise; one did, and the correction STRENGTHENS the finding while WEAKENING the exemplar I\nhad chosen -- which is exactly the asymmetry the rule exists to surface.\n\ngrok's other two calls -- **do not join [[sgirandomcolour]]** (my Q6) and **talk HIGH down to MEDIUM** (my Q5)\n-- are NOT actioned here.  They are adjudication questions and the panel is still running; the\nprovisional-adjudication rule says a ruling short of the roster is provisional on its face, so they wait for\nthe full six.\n\n*** PASS-1 BRIEF DEFECT, RAISED INDEPENDENTLY BY ALL THREE PACKET-FED SEATS: I ASSERTED A PREMISE THEY\nCOULD NOT CHECK, AND SAID `SETTLED PREMISES: NONE CARRIED'. ***\n\nNo burns -- all three returned real verdicts on a 31 KB brief -- and all three named the same hole:\n  * **minimax**: *\"the load-bearing premise is NOT CHECKABLE and the brief states it confidently anyway ...\n    That single fact -- the body of `cpus/memory_rw.c' -- is what would settle it, and the brief should not\n    have stated it as confidently as it did.\"*\n  * **glm**: *\"reachability rests on one uncheckable premise\"*; it treated every grep and scan output in the\n    brief as uncheckable and said so.\n  * **deepseek**: caught a SECOND one I had not noticed -- *\"the brief's table for `default'/YCRCB assumes\n    `fatal()' returns\"*.  My PATH-A table lists the `default:' arm as filling `buf[0..3]' and then writing;\n    **if `fatal()' exited, that arm would never reach the write and the row would be wrong.**\n\n**BOTH RESOLVE IN THE FINDING'S FAVOUR, WHICH IS NOT THE POINT.**  Checked at HEAD: `fatal()' in\n`src/core/debugmsg.c' is `va_start / va_debug / va_end' with **no exit and no early-out**, so the arm does\nreach the write; and grok, a FILE-READING seat, settled the other by reporting the destination is RAM rather\nthan a device, so `#95's pre-zero never runs at all.\n\n**THE DEFECT IS THAT I MADE A DOCUMENTED MISTAKE THE TEMPLATE WARNS ABOUT IN CAPITALS.**  `BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md`\nsection C is headed *\"MANDATORY.  COPY FORWARD.  THIS IS THE ONE THAT GETS DROPPED\"*, and my brief wrote\n*\"SETTLED PREMISES: none carried -- first brief of this campaign\"*.  **That was true about the CAMPAIGN and\nfalse about the QUESTIONS**: the template's own test is whether a seat could build an *if X then A, else B*\nanswer on the premise -- and deepseek built exactly that on `fatal()'.  The obligation is PER-PACKET and\nBROAD, *whatever pass or round settled it*, and `fatal()'-does-not-exit was settled long ago; the carrier\nrecords it costing the minimax seat its answer twice before.\n\n**THE TRANSPORT PREAMBLE DID ITS JOB AND THE BRIEF DID NOT.**  `panel_ollama.py' asks a packet-fed seat to\nNAME any premise it cannot settle instead of guessing, and all three did precisely that rather than\nratifying -- which is the behaviour that makes a packet-fed seat worth firing.  Score this as a BRIEF DEFECT,\nnever as three weak answers.\n\n**FOR THE PASS-2 PACKET:** inline (a) `fatal()'s four-line body, (b) the `memory_rw' pre-zero site with the\nbranch that gates it, and (c) grok's RAM-not-device finding with its warrant.  All three are one or two lines\neach and they are what these seats spent their answers asking for.\n\n*** THE CLASS SCAN'S DENOMINATOR IS WRONG, AND THE WAY IT IS WRONG IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING.  Codex seat,\npass 1, verdict GO-WITH-CHANGES. ***\n\nThe brief claimed SIX unpinned-length sites and FIVE stack-buffer write sites, and concluded that\n`dev_sgi_re.c' is the only member of the class in the device tree.  **Codex found four write sites both of my\nscanners missed**, and named the mechanism for each:\n\n  * `dev_dec21143.c' descriptor writebacks (two sites) -- *\"Nested argument commas defeat the scanner's\n    splitting.\"*  **That is limit 1, which the brief DISCLOSED**, demonstrated on real sites.\n  * `dev_dreamcast_maple.c' response writes (two sites) -- *\"whose casts prevent recognition of the buffer\n    argument.\"*  **That limit was NOT disclosed and I did not know about it.**\n\nAll four are SAFE (initialised via `store_le32', or cast forms that do fill the buffer), **so the CONCLUSION\nsurvives and the DENOMINATOR does not.**  Those are different claims and the brief conflated them.\n\n*** THE METHODOLOGICAL POINT IS SHARPER THAN THE COUNT, AND IT LANDS ON THE BRIEF'S BEST-LOOKING EVIDENCE: ***\n*\"Agreement between scanners sharing parsing limitations is not exhaustive corroboration.\"*  The brief\npresented TWO independent scans agreeing as strong evidence of scope.  **They are not independent** -- both\nsplit `memory_rw' arguments on commas with the same regex, so both are blind to the same call shapes.  Two\nderivers that can fail the same way are ONE deriver run twice, and this project has already recorded that\nexact lesson on the `sgirekills' count, where two agreeing greps both counted comment lines.\n\n**Two of the brief's SAFE verdicts also need narrowing, per the same seat:**\n  * **GBE** -- the divisor is nonzero and the transfer is bounded, but *\"conversion still loops to 512 after a\n    shorter read\"*, which can consume unfilled bytes.  Codex declines to call it a demonstrated guest\n    disclosure (the forwarded framebuffer prefix is smaller) but says it *\"invalidates the blanket\n    initialization-safety claim\"*.  Correct: my check established the LENGTH bound and said nothing about\n    consumption.\n  * **PXG** -- `#207' bounds the later SRAM copy's SOURCE SPAN without bounding CONSUMPTION, and the initial\n    SRAM copy is unchecked.  The enumerated 3080-byte PX maximum stands; what falls is treating it as proof of\n    complete initialisation across BOTH branches.\n\n**Consequence for this round: the scope sentence must be rewritten before pass 2** -- from *\"the only member\nof this class in the device tree\"* to *\"the only DEFECTIVE member among the sites these two scanners can\nsee, whose blind spots are nested-comma argument lists and cast buffer arguments\"*.  The two new correctness\nfindings the same seat raised are filed separately as [[sgirergbroundtrip]] and [[sgimtedepth4dead]].\n\n*** PASS-1 ADJUDICATION.  EVIDENCE BASIS: 6 of 9 -- six scriptable seats ANSWERED (0 walled, 0 down, 0\nburns, novelty 93-100%); agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED on the measurement reserve; opus5 and fable5 HELD for the\n2026-09-09 adjudication by standing owner directive.  The three absences are DIRECTED, not failures -- but the\nprovisional-adjudication rule still applies and this ruling is provisional on its face. ***\n\n**THE FINDING SURVIVES ALL SIX.**  Nobody disputed that `buf' is uninitialised, that the two mode fields are\nindependent, or that `#95' fails to cover the paths.  What changed is the FRAMING, in three places, and every\ncorrection came from a seat rather than from me.\n\n**D1 -- Q6, ONE ROUND OR TWO: SEPARATE.  My proposal REFUTED, and refuted with a measurement.**  Three seats\nsaid separate (grok, kimi, deepseek); kimi proved it: a shared *destination is deterministic across runs*\noracle is **VACUOUS for this row**, because `srandom(tv.tv_sec ^ getpid() ^ tv.tv_usec)' makes `random()' vary\nper run while STALE-STACK REUSE UNDER A FIXED GUEST PROGRAM IS RUN-DETERMINISTIC -- so the buggy build PASSES.\nVerified at `src/core/main.c:688-691'.  **I asked the panel not to let the convenience of one round drive the\nanswer, and it did not.**  ACCEPTED: two rounds.\n\n**D2 -- Q5, SEVERITY: grok says MEDIUM, kimi says HIGH *conditionally*.  RESOLVED BY KIMI'S CONDITION, not by\ncounting.**  kimi: HIGH is defensible for this fork's mandate *'provided the entry carries the corrected\nreachability framing ... Do not ship the entry with \"no undocumented value anywhere\" in it.'*  The row KEEPS\nHIGH and the framing is corrected here.  grok's MEDIUM rests on disclosure impact, which is genuinely weak (4\nstale bytes per pixel, usually prior pixels); kimi's HIGH rests on the fork's mandate and on this defect\nfalsifying `#95's *closes the whole class* sentence in the same batch that edited this array.  **That is the\nstronger warrant for THIS fork**, and it is the one I take.  Recorded as a SPLIT, not as agreement.\n\n**F1 -- A FALSE SENTENCE IN MY BRIEF, and it is NOT being edited.**  The brief says the paths are reached\n*'with no undocumented value anywhere'*.  **False**: PATH A's MAXIMAL form (`bufdepth' 8) needs the\nundocumented depth field 3.  Documented forms exist -- CI x BUFDEPTH_16/32 for PATH A, and PATH B entirely --\nso the FINDING stands while the blanket phrase does not.  **The sent brief is left BYTE-UNCHANGED on purpose:\nit is the frozen packet six seats reviewed, and rewriting it would falsify the record of what was asked.**\nThe correction lives here and goes into the pass-2 packet.\n\n**F2 -- THE WITNESS IS NOT `ONE CONSTANT'.**  kimi: it needs a register plus the `step_x' twin, or ZERO extra\nregisters via a PUT-only RECT path.  My cost claim was optimistic; the PUT-only path is the cheaper design and\nwas not in the brief at all.\n\n**F3 -- INDEPENDENT CONVERGENCE ON A NEW DEFECT.**  codex and kimi BOTH found the dead `if (depth == 4)'\nwithout knowledge of each other.  Independent convergence is the one kind of agreement that IS evidence, so\n[[sgimtedepth4dead]] ships with two witnesses to its existence.\n\n**OWED BEFORE PASS 2**, from §C2 and the three packet-fed seats who each asked for it: inline `fatal()'s body,\nthe `memory_rw' pre-zero site with its gating branch, and grok's RAM-not-device finding with its warrant.\n\n*** UNDER-HARVESTED REVIEW, CORRECTED 2026-09-07.  I READ THIS SEAT'S ANSWER FOR ITS NEW FINDINGS AND\nSTOPPED.  IT ALSO REFUTED FOUR OF MY CLAIMS AND **REJECTED MY WITNESS DESIGN**, AND I RECORDED NONE OF IT.\nTHIS IS THE UNREAD-REVIEW CLASS, ON AN ANSWER I HAD ALREADY OPENED. (regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/codex.txt) ***\n\n**1. MY WITNESS DESIGN IS REJECTED, and the reason is architectural rather than a detail.**  I proposed\npainting a first pixel so `buf' holds KNOWN bytes, then reading a second at a mismatched depth and asserting\nthe result carries bytes from the first.  codex: *\"Separate automatic objects have no architectural promise\nof retaining each other's contents.  Register allocation, inlining, and intervening calls matter.\"*  And the\nsame objection kills the other direction: *\"**Path A has the same problem:** observing an unassigned byte\ndoes not guarantee that its value differs from the intended padding.\"*  **I flagged that risk myself in the\nbrief -- `an oracle of \"the value is wrong\" can pass by luck' -- proposed a fix for it, and did not record\nthat the fix was refused.**\n\n**The prescribed measurement instead: an ATTRIBUTED INITIALIZEDNESS RESULT per direction** -- MemorySanitizer\nor Valgrind Memcheck -- *\"tied to the reviewed build, together with passing functional controls\"*, with the\ncaveat that *\"merely copying undefined bytes need not produce an immediate diagnostic\"*, so instrumentation\ncoverage must be established rather than assumed.  **That is a different kind of round from the one I was\nplanning** and it is why this row is not next in the queue.\n\n**2. `LIN_A cannot be refused' IS FALSE.**  I wrote that case 4 FORCES the valid bit so the arm is reached\nunconditionally.  codex: *\"remove 'LIN_A cannot be refused': its index guard can return before the buffer\ncode.\"*  `tile_nr = x >> 12; if (tile_nr >= 32) return;` -- an early return I read past.\n\n**3. `THE WITNESS IS ONE CONSTANT CHANGED' IS FALSE.**  *\"The existing probe uses its LIN_A constant for the\ndestination and matched control as well as a source case.  Changing that shared constant changes the\nobservation path and the control simultaneously.\"*  My cost estimate was wrong for the same reason kimi's\ncorrection said so, and I recorded kimi's and not this one.\n\n**4. `UNINITIALISED ON EVERY PATH' OVERSTATES IT.**  *\"matched operations initialize every byte they\nconsume.\"*  The defect is the MISMATCHED pairs, not the buffer in general.\n\n**5. MY `WORSE THAN I SAID' CLAIM ABOUT RAM IS WRONG.**  I recorded that grok's RAM-not-device finding makes\nboth paths worse.  codex: *\"**RAM does not inherently make Path B worse:** a successful RAM read defines the\nrequested prefix through `memcpy'; the missing suffix is the same problem.\"*\n\n**6. NOT ALL SEVEN CALL SITES TAKE THE GUEST MODE WORDS** -- *\"the two MTE sites construct their own modes\"*.\nMy reachability paragraph implies all seven.\n\n**7. SEVERITY: MED, not HIGH.**  *\"HIGH is not established by inheriting #95's severity.\"*  And it refuses\nthe downgrade too: *\"'usually the previous pixel' and 'first call in a frame' are unsupported explanations of\nstack contents.  They cannot justify downgrading this to a cosmetic rendering defect.\"*  **Both of my\nseverity arguments were unsupported, in opposite directions.**  Lowered to MED provisionally, with potential\nhost information disclosure recorded as unestablished rather than assumed either way.\n\n*** THE PROCESS FAILURE IS THE POINT AND IT IS THE THIRD INSTANCE TONIGHT. ***  I opened this file, extracted\nthe two NEW defects and the census correction, and stopped -- because new findings are what I was looking\nfor.  **A seat that refutes your premises is easy to under-read precisely because its corrections are not\nphrased as discoveries.**  The same failure hit the `lcabadval` answer (an eleventh site missed) and the\n`alphaswpctxptbr` premises.  All three were answers already on disk.\n\n*** THE STRONGEST REACHABILITY VEHICLE WAS IN THE KIMI ANSWER ALL ALONG, AND IT IS ALL-DOCUMENTED.\nHarvested and verified 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/kimi.txt). ***\n\nEverything above argues reachability from the DE path and concedes that PATH A's maximal form needs the\nundocumented depth field 3.  **There is a third vehicle that needs nothing undocumented at all**, and it is\nthe one a real driver uses: `do_mte_transfer()' constructs its own modes with NO colour bits (so CI), because\nthe `if (depth == 4)' that would set RGBA is dead code -- see [[sgimtedepth4dead]], raised to HIGH on this.\n\nCI fills only `buf[0]'; `MTE_DEPTH_16' and `MTE_DEPTH_32' are documented and give `bufdepth' 2 and 4.  **So\nevery MTE fill at those depths leaks 1 or 3 uninitialised bytes per pixel, with every value documented**, on\nthe path kimi names as used by `crmfb_fill_rect'.\n\n**This also settles the `not all seven call sites take the guest mode words' correction** recorded above from\nthe codex seat: the two MTE sites construct their own modes -- and constructing them is exactly what makes\nthem leak.  The two corrections are the same fact seen from opposite sides, and I recorded one without\nnoticing it implied the other.\n\n**AND BOTH FILE-READING SEATS INDEPENDENTLY REACHED THE DETECTOR REQUIREMENT:** the oracle must assert\nVALUES, not determinism.  kimi's proof is the sharper one -- post-fix PATH B yields a deterministic value AND\nthe `bufdepth'-sized mutant yields the previous pixel's bytes, which are *also* deterministic under stack\nreuse, so *\"a determinism oracle CANNOT catch the mutant\"*.  Two seats, two routes, one conclusion.\n\n*** FOURTH UNDER-HARVESTED ANSWER, AND THE RICHEST PER BYTE: THE GROK SEAT'S 15 KB\n(regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/grok.txt).  IT ANSWERED THE OPTION-SET QUESTION WITH GREPPED IN-TREE PRECEDENT, NAMED\nTHREE MEMBERS I HAD NOT, ARGUED THE DISPOSITION I WAS LEANING TOWARD IS WRONG, AND FOUND THE MTE CHAIN\nINDEPENDENTLY.  I HAD RECORDED ONLY ITS BUFDEPTH CORRECTION. ***\n\n**1. THE MTE CHAIN, INDEPENDENTLY.**  *\"16- and 32-bit MTE TLB fills therefore always run as CI + matching\nbufdepth.  32-bit fill writes `bg''s low byte plus three uninit bytes.  Comment at :1322 says NetBSD\n`crmfb_fill_rect' uses this.\"*  **So TWO seats found it and I missed it from both** -- and grok additionally\npinned the in-file comment naming the driver, which I had attributed to kimi alone.\n\n**Its fix-order point is sharper than the one I recorded:** *\"Do NOT join: fixing `== 4' to `== 32' changes\nMTE 32-bit COLOUR semantics, which memset does not.  Memset would turn that path into 'low byte + zeros' --\ndefined, still wrong.\"*  I had noted that repairing the dead branch alone MASKS the leak; grok's is the dual\nand it matters as much -- **the memset fix leaves the MTE path DEFINED AND STILL WRONG**, because the colour\nsemantics are a separate defect from the initialisation.  Neither fix subsumes the other.\n\n**2. THREE OPTION-SET MEMBERS I DID NOT NAME**, and it reached them by grepping rather than reasoning:\n    6.  `uint8_t buf[8] = {0};`  -- the C99 form, same observable as a memset\n    7.  CLAMP THE TRANSFER to bytes filled (`len = min(bufdepth, filled)`) -- guest-visible difference: the\n        OLD DESTINATION BYTES SURVIVE, which is neither zeros nor stale stack\n    8.  IMPLEMENT the modes (YCRCB, 64-bit depth) -- and it refused this itself on the ADDITION LICENCE: no\n        CRIME DE pixel-packing datasheet exists in `_scratchpad/`\n\n**3. THE IN-TREE PRECEDENTS, WHICH IS WHAT THE OPTION-SET RULE ASKS FOR AND I DID NOT DO:**\n    * **this function's own RGB PUT already pads with zero** (`buf[3] = 0`) -- in-function precedent for\n      DEFINED PADDING rather than refusal;\n    * `dev_dreamcast_maple.c' does `memset(buf, 0, 8)' then a partial fill then `MEM_WRITE' -- **the same\n      shape, and the house answer there is zero the whole array**;\n    * `dev_sgi_gbe.c' (`#196') renders an unimplemented colour type as BLACK -- defined zeros, no `fatal'\n      flood;\n    * MTE's own `zerobuf' is memset before its write, in this same file.\n\n**4. IT ARGUES DECLINE IS THE WRONG DISPOSITION, AND THE REASON TIES BACK TO THE MTE CHAIN:**\n*\"`CI' + `BUFDEPTH_32' is a plausible 8-bit index in a 32-bit container (RGB already pads).  Declining it\nwould also break the MTE 16/32-bit TLB fill path, which ALREADY EMITS THAT PAIR.  No hardware evidence it is\nillegal.\"*  **That is decisive against member 4** and I had not connected the two facts -- the very pair a\ndecline would reject is the one the model's own MTE path generates.\n\nIts recommendation is **(1)/(6), full-object zero**, with the comment saying plainly that hardware padding is\nunknown and this is defined behaviour rather than a CRIME model -- which is also what the codex seat\nindependently chose.\n\n*** 5. AN APPARENT DISAGREEMENT I COLLAPSED, RESOLVED HERE BY READING BOTH RATHER THAN PICKING ONE. ***\nI recorded codex's *\"RAM does not inherently make PATH B worse\"* as a correction of my summary.  grok says\nthe opposite in its heading: *\"PATH B is worse than the brief said.\"*  **Reading both: they are not in\nconflict.**  grok's point is that `#95' does not run AT ALL on a RAM destination, so nothing pre-zeroes\nanything; codex's is that a successful RAM read still DEFINES the requested prefix through `memcpy', so only\nthe suffix beyond `bufdepth' is undefined -- which is the same defect either way.  **codex is right on the\ntechnical point and grok is right about the brief's framing.**  Recording only one of them, as I did, loses\nthat.\n\n*** THE THREE PRECEDENTS VERIFIED, AND THE THIRD IS THIS PROJECT'S OWN PRIOR RULING ON THE SAME\nQUESTION.  Checked 2026-09-07 -- grok said `grep, not argument', so they were grepped. ***\n\n    buf[3] = 0;                         dev_sgi_re.c, this function's own RGB PUT      CONFIRMED\n    memset(buf, 0, 8);                  dev_dreamcast_maple.c, twice (:285, :330)      CONFIRMED\n    *r = *g = *b = 0;                   dev_sgi_gbe.c #196                             CONFIRMED\n\n**The `#196' comment is the decisive one and it is BY THIS PROJECT'S OWN SEATS:**\n\n    default:/*  #196: (Codex/Fable) d->color_mode is guest-controlled; an\n                unimplemented WID mode must not exit() the host (get_rgb is\n                per-pixel, so don't fatal()-flood either) -- render black.  */\n            *r = *g = *b = 0;\n\n**That is the same question already answered**: a GUEST-CONTROLLED unimplemented colour mode, in a PER-PIXEL\nhelper, on a SIBLING SGI framebuffer -- and the ruling was DEFINED ZEROS, with the per-pixel property given\nexplicitly as the reason not to `fatal()'-flood.  It is the identical reasoning `sgirandompixel' used for its\nlatch, so the house doctrine is consistent across three rounds.\n\n**SO THE DISPOSITION IS SETTLED BY TRANSFERRED WARRANT RATHER THAN BY PREFERENCE:** full-object zero, with a\ncomment saying plainly that hardware padding is unknown and this is defined behaviour rather than a CRIME\nmodel.  Both file-reading seats reached it independently, and `#196' is a fourth vote from a round that\nalready shipped.\n\n*This is the option-set rule working exactly as written -- `GREP THE FORK FOR AN EXISTING IDIOM; the tree's\nown precedents are part of the design space'.  I did not do that; grok did, and it produced a better answer\nthan the argument I was assembling.*\n\n*** FIFTH UNDER-HARVESTED ANSWER -- THE PACKET-FED SEATS.  glm's contains a DEFECT NOBODY ELSE FOUND\n(filed as [[sgilinearnegidx]]) and a BETTER VERSION OF MY OWN #95 ARGUMENT. ***\n\n**glm's `#95' warrant is stronger than the one I wrote and does not depend on reading the source:**\n*\"zeroing past `len' would itself be an OOB write on any caller with a tight buffer ... So every\nMEMORY-SAFE implementation of `#95' zeroes at most `bufdepth' bytes, and PATH B's `buf[bufdepth..3]' are\nstale under all of them, not merely the described one.  PATH A is a `MEM_WRITE' whose source is `buf'; a\nwrite-side pre-zero cannot exist there without destroying the payload of every device write in the tree.\nSurvival against `#95' is OVER-DETERMINED, not lucky.\"*  My version rested on the pre-zero happening to be\ngated on `MEM_READ'; glm's shows no memory-safe implementation could behave otherwise.\n\n**And a correction to my own §3b:** I wrote that `#95' zeroes `buf[0..bufdepth-1]' unconditionally.  glm:\n*\"that is the device branch only -- the stronger case presented as fact, harmless to the claim.\"*\n\n**minimax's witness-table point, recorded with its own arithmetic slip corrected:** it observes that the\nwitness table lists only RGBA mode words and omits the CI cases, which is fair -- CI leaks at any depth above\n1 and no row drives it.  Its example value is wrong, though: it calls `0x1210' *LIN_A | depth 2 | CI*, and\n`0x1210 & 0xf0' is `0x10' = RGB, not CI (`0x00').  **The observation stands and the constant does not.**\n\n*** SHARPENED 2026-09-07 BY THE glm SEAT on the `sgirergbroundtrip` pass-1 panel (_scratchpad/panel_20260907_051055), and it is\nwider than this row said. ***\n\n    \"per the code's own #100 comment a bufdepth of 8 makes every named PUT case transmit four\n     uninitialized stack bytes to guest memory; a bufdepth-8 GET silently drops the high half;\n     and with bufdepth 1 or 2 the RGB/RGBA/ABGR GETs read uninitialized buf[2..3].\"\n\n**THREE DISTINCT EXPOSURES, not one**, and the row had only the first:\n  (a) bufdepth 8 PUT -- `buf[4..7]` are never written by ANY arm, so four uninitialised stack bytes\n      go to guest memory through `memory_rw(..., MEM_WRITE)`;\n  (b) bufdepth 8 GET -- the high half is read into buf and then silently discarded, since every GET\n      arm reads at most four bytes.  Not a leak; a correctness gap;\n  (c) **bufdepth 1 or 2 GET -- the RGB, RGBA and ABGR arms read `buf[2..3]` that `memory_rw` never\n      filled.**  This is the one the row did not have, and it is the mirror of (a): a LEAK INTO THE\n      GUEST'S OWN PIXEL VALUE rather than into guest memory.\n\n**THE GUARD/BODY SPLIT IS WHY ALL THREE EXIST:** the colour type is bits 4-7 of the mode word and\n`bufdepth` is bits 8-9 of the SAME word, read independently.  Nothing couples them, so a guest\nselects a four-byte colour type with a one-byte transfer length whenever it likes.\n\n*Recorded from `#505`'s pre-table GUARD-FIELD-vs-BODY-FIELD check, which found the same disagreeing\nstate independently and then DECLINED to row it -- the exposure is identical before and after that\nround's edit (the shipped GET read three bytes, the fixed one reads three bytes), so a row there\nwould attribute a pre-existing defect to a round that neither created nor cured it.*\n\n*** PASS-1 DESIGN AUDIT, 2026-09-07 -- BY grok, BECAUSE CODEX REFUSED THE BRIEF.\n`regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/grok_pass1_20260907.txt`, 18 kB. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"GO-WITH-CHANGES.  Zero-fill `buf` at its current declaration is the right fix for\nTHIS class and is the whole fix OF this class.  It is not the packing fix, it is not PUT-only, and\nno single-access guest value of 0 can pin it.  S1 and S5 hold.  S2's decode holds.  S3's GET\noverstates RGB.  S4's quotes exist and its site-count does not.\"*\n\n**Q1/Q2 ANSWERED, and Q2's cost objection dissolved by a fact I did not have:** *\"It is declared\nAFTER the coordinate / `tlb_mode` / `tileptr`-valid early returns, so a zero-fill at that site is not\npaid on declined pixels.\"*  So the per-pixel cost applies only to pixels that were going to be\ntransferred anyway -- which removes the reason to prefer a narrower site.\n\n**Q3 ANSWERED, and it is the answer I asked for rather than the one I wanted:** *\"no single-access\nguest value of `0` can pin it.\"*  A value oracle cannot distinguish \"filled with zeros\" from\n\"happened to be zeros\", which is what the brief suspected and what the round must now design around.\n\n*** MY S3 WAS WRONG IN ITS EMPHASIS, AND grok's ENUMERATION IS BETTER THAN MY PROSE. ***  I led with\ndepth 8; the severe case is CI:\n\n    PUT bytes transferred with no store:\n                              depth 1   depth 2   depth 4     depth 8\n      CI                      none      buf[1]    buf[1..3]   buf[1..7]\n      RGB/RGBA/ABGR/default   none      none      none        buf[4..7]\n\n**CI at depth 4 already transfers three unstored bytes**, which needs no unusual depth at all -- and\nthe MTE path forces CI (see [[sgimtedepth4dead]]).  *\"emphasis on depth 8 is the weak vehicle.\"*\n\n**S2 REFINED, and the refinement matters for how this is described:** on the MTE path the colour bits\nare NOT a second guest field.  `src_mode`/`dst_mode` are built as `(tlb << 10) + (depth_field << 8)`\nwith no type bits, so `color_mode` is pinned at 0 (CI) unless the dead branch fires.  *\"That is a\nconstant colliding with a guest depth, not two independent guest fields.\"*  The two-independent-fields\nstory is true of the DE path and false of the MTE path.\n\n*** TWO NEW FINDINGS UNDER THE FOUND-SOMETHING-ELSE LICENCE: ***\n  1. **`DE_MODE_PIXDEPTH_*` (bits 2-3) is never read by `dev_sgi_re.c` at all.**  A third mode field\n     the device ignores entirely.\n  2. **A WRONG RECORD IN PRODUCT CODE, verified by reading:** `dev_sgi_re.c:732` says\n     `// bufdepth = 1, 2, or 4.` immediately above\n     `int src_bufdepth = 1 << ((src_mode >> 8) & 3);` -- which yields **1, 2, 4 or 8**.  The `#100`\n     round grew `buf[8]` precisely because the decode reaches 8, so this comment was already false\n     when that round shipped.  **L4, cheap, and it belongs to whichever round next touches this\n     file.**\n\n**S4's SITE COUNT IS WRONG AND ITS QUOTES ARE NOT.**  I wrote that `memory_rw.c` zero-fills at three\nsites; grok says the count does not hold and flagged in its own trace that *\"GET versus PUT may not\nshare a `#95` path\"*.  **That distinction is load-bearing and I had glossed it:** `#95` zero-fills a\nREAD buffer for `len` bytes on a FAILED access.  It therefore does nothing for a PUT (a write), and\non a successful GET it fills exactly `bufdepth` bytes -- never `buf[2..3]` beyond them.  **So the\ntransferred warrant is weaker than the brief claimed: `#95` establishes the fork's ETHOS for this\nclass, not a mechanism that already covers these calls.**\n\n*Recorded as the round's own premise-audit result: of five settled premises, two survived intact, one\nwas refined, one had its emphasis corrected, and one had its supporting count refuted.*\n\n*** A SECOND, INDEPENDENT AUDIT AGREES ON THE FIX AND GOES FURTHER ON THE ORACLE.  codex, via the\nREWORDED brief (`codex_reworded_20260907.txt`) after the original was refused. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"Zero-initialize all eight bytes at the existing declaration.  That completes the local\ninitialization repair in both directions, but does not establish correct pixel-format or MTE\nbehavior.  **No guest-visible value or write-count oracle can reliably prove initialization\noccurred.**\"*\n\n**TWO SEATS, TWO TRANSPORTS, SAME FIX.**  grok: *\"Zero-fill `buf` at its current declaration is the\nright fix for THIS class and is the whole fix OF this class.\"*  codex: *\"Zero-initialize all eight\nbytes at the existing declaration.\"*  Both add the same caveat -- it repairs initialisation and does\nNOT repair the packing/format defect, which is [[sgimtedepth4dead]]'s territory.  **The design is\nsettled.**\n\n*** THE ORACLE IS HARDER THAN THE NEIGHBOURING ROUND ASSUMED, AND THIS IS THE LOAD-BEARING FINDING.\n***  grok said no single-access guest VALUE can pin it.  codex rules out the WRITE-COUNT oracle as\nwell -- and a write-count oracle is exactly what a seat proposed for [[sgirandomcolour]]\n(*\"observe ZERO MEM_WRITE calls from an unsupported PUT\"*).  **That proposal survives for\n`sgirandomcolour`, where the question is whether a write HAPPENS; it does not transfer to this row,\nwhere the question is what the written bytes CONTAIN.**  Two rounds, one idiom, and it is valid in\nonly one of them.\n\n**CONSEQUENCE FOR THE ROUND'S SHAPE:** if no black-box guest-visible oracle can prove\ninitialisation, this round's detector cannot be a rung-3 probe of the ordinary kind.  The candidates\nare an offline differential over the arms, or a `harness:` witness under the ladder's fifth term.\n**That must be decided BEFORE the fix, not after** -- the round is otherwise a one-line edit with no\nway to defend it.\n\n**AND THE CEILING FOR THIS ROW IS NOW STATED CLEANLY** (same answer, Q1): *\"initializing the buffer\nchanges neither the transfer length nor the call count.  Uninitialized bytes can happen to match the\nexpected initialized values, so even matching values PLUS matching counts cannot establish\ninitialization.  **Unexpected values can expose a failure; expected values cannot prove\ndefinedness.**\"*\n\nThat is the sharpest form of the limit and it settles the round's shape: **no guest-visible oracle\ncan prove this fix worked.**  The detector must be an offline differential or a `harness:` witness\nunder the ladder's fifth term -- decided BEFORE the one-line edit, not after.\n\n*** FULL PANEL 2026-09-07 (`_scratchpad/panel_20260907_130159`).  SIX SEATS, ZERO DOWN, glm did\nNOT burn for the second panel running. ***\n\n**THE SPLIT, which is the thing for the adjudication:**\n  * **grok: TAKE THE ROUND** -- *\"zero-fill `buf` at the existing declaration; do not make the arms\n    write `bufdepth` bytes; do not treat a guest 0 as proof of initialisation.\"*\n  * **deepseek: NOT RATIFY as a correctness fix** -- *\"zero-fill only converts uninitialized reads\n    into defined-but-wrong bytes for CI depth>1; the independent assignment/transfer defect\n    remains.\"*\n  * **minimax: the symptom is handled but THE LICENCE IS UNSUPPORTED** -- *\"no measured delta is\n    offered.\"*\n\n  *So the question is not whether to zero-fill.  It is whether zero-fill is a FIX or a SYMPTOM PATCH\n  that leaves the real decoupling defect in place -- and whether the addition licence permits it with\n  no measured delta.*\n\n*** THREE SEATS INDEPENDENTLY FOUND A DEFECT IN MY PACKET, AND IT IS A CROSS-ROUND INTERACTION I HAD\nNOT SEEN. ***  S3's REVERSE-DIRECTION sentence -- *\"at depth 1 or 2 the read fills only that many\nbytes and the multi-byte arms then read `buf[2..3]` regardless\"* -- is **false of RGB as the file now\nsits**.  grok: *\"its GET sentence is false of RGB.\"*  glm: *\"asserted in prose, not in the enumerated\ntable, and under the packet's own model it is wrong.\"*  minimax: *\"S3's table is asymmetric -- write\ndirection enumerated, read direction mentioned in one sentence only.\"*\n\n  **WHY: `#505` changed RGB GET from `buf[1..3]` to `buf[0..2]`.**  So post-`#505` an RGB read at\n  depth 2 touches `buf[2]` and NOT `buf[3]`.  The sentence was true of the pre-`#505` file and I\n  carried it forward into a packet written after the fix.  **My own two rounds interact and the\n  enumerated table hid it, because I enumerated the WRITE direction and prosed the READ direction.**\n  *A table beside a sentence invites exactly this: the table gets checked and the sentence does not.*\n\n**THREE SEATS ALSO AGREE S4's WARRANT IS DEAD:** *\"S4 is ethos, not precedent\"* (deepseek, minimax);\n*\"its site-count and the `#95` transfer do not [hold]\"* (grok).  I had already recorded `#95` as\nweaker than claimed; the panel makes it unanimous.  **The addition licence therefore wants a MEASURED\nDELTA for this one-line change, and none exists.**  That is now the round's blocking question rather\nthan the oracle.\n\n**Q1 IS SETTLED AGAINST DEPTH-AWARE ARMS**, by two seats and for the same reason: *\"depth-aware arms\nwould trespass on the neighbouring rows that own pixel semantics and would require hardware truth\nthis packet does not contain\"* (kimi).  So the boundary with [[sgimtedepth4dead]] and\n[[sgirandomcolour]] holds.\n\n*** AND THE ORACLE QUESTION HAS ITS FIRST CONSTRUCTIVE ANSWER, from the kimi substitute: ***\n*\"The only honest definedness detector is an instrumented run (MSan/Valgrind class); any harness\nwitness is one-sided **UNLESS THE STACK BENEATH THE FRAME IS PRE-SEEDED**.\"*\n\n  **That is actionable and nobody had proposed it.**  Every earlier answer said no oracle can prove\n  definedness; this says a harness witness becomes TWO-SIDED if the frame's stack is seeded with a\n  known non-zero pattern first -- then `0` means initialised and the pattern means not.  This fork\n  already has an ASAN/UBSan tree (`build-asan/`), so the instrumented route is also available.\n  **Both need checking before the round; neither was on the table an hour ago.**\n\n*** THE CODEX SEAT REFUTED S4 AND THE OTHER THREE SEATS RATIFIED MY ERROR.  SETTLED BY OPENING\nTHE FILE, NOT BY COUNTING SEATS. ***\n\nMy S4 said `#95` *\"fills a READ buffer for `len` bytes on a FAILED access\"*.  deepseek, minimax and\ngrok all agreed and graded the warrant *\"ethos, not precedent\"*.  **codex went and read it:**\n\n    memory_rw.c :492   if (writeflag == MEM_READ) memset(data, 0, len);\n                :494   paddr -= mem->devices[i].baseaddr;      <- THE DEVICE DISPATCH\n\n**`:492` is not in a failure branch at all.**  It prefills on EVERY device read, successful or not,\nbefore dispatch and before length clamping -- its own comment says *\"never hand back uninitialised\nhost memory to the guest.  Root-cause fix for the uninit-read class\"*.  The failure-path fills are\n`:215` and `:267`, the two that `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED`.  **Verified here by reading all six\nsites.**  codex's enumeration of six (215, 267, 492, 671, 683, 732) is correct and my 'three sites'\nwas wrong as well.\n\n  > **SO THE WARRANT IS A PRECEDENT, NOT AN ETHOS: `#95` is exactly 'initialise storage before a\n  > producer may assign only part of it'.**  That is this fix, one layer down.  The addition licence\n  > is satisfied by TRANSFERRED WARRANT and the round does NOT owe a measured delta -- which\n  > REVERSES the blocking question three seats had just created.\n\n**THIS IS THE WRONG-FACT-COMES-BACK-CONFIRMED CLASS AT FULL STRENGTH.**  I stated a weakened S4 as a\nSETTLED PREMISE; three of six seats confirmed it; one seat checked it.  The carrier's rule --\n*unanimity on a brief-supplied fact is worth nothing; score agreement only where a seat re-derived\nit* -- scores this exactly right, and I had written the premise-audit opener into the brief and\nstill stated S4 as settled rather than as a question.\n\n*The sharper lesson: **a premise I had ALREADY CORRECTED ONCE, in the weakening direction, is the\nmost dangerous kind.**  It arrives wearing the clothes of a correction, so it reads as the careful\nversion and nobody re-checks it -- including me, who wrote it.*\n\n**codex's other corrections, each verified before recording:**\n  * **S1 is wrong.**  Not 'once per pixel from three callers': THREE execution paths, TWO caller\n    functions, SEVEN direct call sites (LINE GET :817 PUT :863; RECTANGLE GET :878, GET :883,\n    PUT :929; tiled MTE GET :1382 PUT :1387).  RECTANGLE does TWO GETs and a PUT per iteration, so\n    'once per pixel' understates a fill's cost threefold on that path.\n  * **S3's GET row corrected, agreeing with grok, glm and minimax** -- per-arm and per-width: CI\n    consumes NO unassigned byte at any width; RGB consumes `buf[1..2]` after a 1-byte read and\n    `buf[2]` after 2; RGBA/ABGR/default consume `buf[1..3]` and `buf[2..3]`.  **`#505` is why RGB\n    differs** -- it now reads `buf[0..2]` and never `buf[3]`.\n  * *** `-DNDEBUG` IS SET, so an `assert()`-based detector is a NO-OP in a normal build. ***\n    Verified: `configure:313` sets `CFLAGS=\"-DNDEBUG $CFLAGS\"`, and `src/devices/Makefile:9` carries\n    it.  An initialisedness harness built on `assert` would be VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION -- the\n    check-that-cannot-fail shape, arriving inside the detector for this very round.\n  * **Q4, a coverage gap in the SHIPPED `#505` detector:** `rgb depth64` and `rgb tiled64` widen the\n    *source read*; their destination stays a four-byte RGBA write, so **no row establishes\n    initialisation of an eight-byte PUT's tail.**  Filed as [[sgireput8tail]] rather than fixed.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:30700:   "title": "sgi_re_width_probe.py's contract states an obligation ('MUST BE GATE-WIRED -- unwired, that coverage exists nowhere') that was DISCHARGED in the same commit that wrote it",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:30703:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07 while reading the file to prep [[sgirekills]].  Its `other decl' contract closes with:\n\n    Those live in sgi_re_unmapped_probe.py (`wr lat1', `rd lat1', `rd decl'),\n    WHICH MUST BE GATE-WIRED -- unwired, that coverage exists nowhere.\n\n**MEASURED: it IS wired, and has been since the commit that wrote the sentence.**\n`git log -S'sgi_re_unmapped_probe.py' -- regress/gate_arm.sh' returns `9f666a8 #499 sgireunmapped', the same\ncommit; `gate_arm.sh' runs the probe and its row list checks all three named rows by name; the block reports\n50 rows emitted, 50 green, 0 red.\n\n**So a reader of that contract today concludes coverage `exists nowhere' when it is wired and green.**  That\nis the carrier's own present-tense-description class -- *a description of a tool, written in the commit that\nchanges the tool, is stale before anyone reads it* -- and it is worse than an ordinary stale line because it\nreads as an OUTSTANDING OBLIGATION, so a future round may re-do work that is done.\n\n**LOW because it misleads rather than mis-measures**: no oracle depends on the sentence.  Repair is to DEMOTE\nit in place (quote it, record that `9f666a8' discharged it) rather than delete, since the reasoning -- that\nunwired coverage exists nowhere -- is exactly why the wiring was done.\n\n**NOT FIXED ON THE SPOT DELIBERATELY.**  The gate was running and about to execute this very probe.  Python\nreads a script whole at startup, so an edit between runs hands two runs different instruments -- the\ncarrier's comparison-run hazard.  A records-only comment change would be invisible in the diff and visible in\nthe measurement, which is the worst combination."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:30722:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  **FOUND BY THE CODEX SEAT** on an unrelated brief, and VERIFIED at HEAD before filing.\n\n    int depth = 8 << ((mode & MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK) >> MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT);\n\n`MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK' is `0x00000300' and `MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT' is 8, so the extracted field is TWO BITS, 0..3,\nand `depth' takes exactly the values **8, 16, 32, 64**.  The function then tests `if (depth == 4)', which is\ntherefore **never true** -- the guarded block is dead code.\n\ncodex's reading of what is lost: the dead block is *\"its intended RGBA construction\"*, so *\"its helper calls\nretain CI\"* -- i.e. the transfer runs in colour-index mode where the author meant RGBA.  **That second half\nis NOT verified here** and is the part a round would have to establish; what IS verified is that the branch\ncannot execute.\n\n*** IT ALSO REFUTES A PREMISE OF THE `sgirebufuninit' BRIEF, which is why it was filed rather than noted. ***\nThat brief asks whether callers constrain the (colour type, depth) pair.  Here is a caller that TRIES to and\nFAILS SILENTLY -- so the model's own producer does not supply the pair its author intended.  Any round\nreasoning about what callers guarantee must account for this one.\n\nLOW because a dead branch cannot itself corrupt anything; the SEVERITY lives in whatever the missing RGBA\nconstruction was supposed to do, which is unestablished.\n\n*** RAISED LOW -> HIGH.  I FILED THIS SAYING `a dead branch cannot itself corrupt anything' AND THAT IS\nWRONG: THE DEAD BRANCH IS WHAT MAKES THE MTE PATH LEAK.  Found in the kimi seat's pass-1 answer\n(regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/kimi.txt) -- which I had already opened and under-read -- and VERIFIED here. ***\n\n    src_mode = (src_tlb << 10) + (((mode & MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK) >> MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT) << 8);\n    dst_mode = (dst_tlb << 10) + (((mode & MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK) >> MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT) << 8);\n    if (depth == 4) {                     <- DEAD: depth = 8 << field, so 8/16/32/64, never 4\n            src_mode |= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA;\n            dst_mode |= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA;\n    }\n\n**The constructed modes carry NO colour bits, so the colour type is 0x00 = CI.**  The only thing that would\nmake them RGBA is the dead branch.  So **the MTE path always runs CI** -- and CI's PUT arm fills ONLY\n`buf[0]' while the transfer is `bufdepth' bytes.\n\n    MTE_DEPTH_8  = 0  -> bufdepth 1   matched, no leak\n    MTE_DEPTH_16 = 1  -> bufdepth 2   fills buf[0], writes 2  -> 1 uninitialised byte per pixel\n    MTE_DEPTH_32 = 2  -> bufdepth 4   fills buf[0], writes 4  -> 3 uninitialised bytes per pixel\n\n**All three are DOCUMENTED constants in `crmfbreg.h'.**  kimi: *\"every MTE_TLB_A/B/C fill with MTE_DEPTH_16/32\nleaks buf[1..3] per pixel (documented MTE_DEPTH values!).  This is the strongest reachability evidence:\nPROM/NetBSD crmfb_fill_rect uses this path.\"*\n\n**SO THE FIX ORDER MATTERS AND IS NOT WHAT I ASSUMED.**  Repairing `depth == 4' ALONE would switch the MTE\npath to RGBA, which fills `buf[0..3]' -- accidentally masking the leak at depth 32 while leaving depth 16 (2\nbytes, RGBA fills 4) and every DE-path case untouched.  **A round that fixes only this would look like it\nfixed [[sgirebufuninit]] and would not have.**  That is the shape the vacuity floor exists to catch, arriving\nbefore either round starts.\n\n*** WHOLE-FUNCTION AUDIT, codex 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/sgimtedepth4dead/codex_audit_20260907.txt).  MY Q4 CONCLUSION IS PARTLY\nWRONG AND THE SCOPE RULING IS FINER THAN `SEQUENCE, DO NOT SHARE'. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"Q1 is correct for the tiled transfer path.  Q4 is partly wrong: correcting the condition for\n32-bit transfers leaves depth 16 in CI.  Additional defects exist; the current `zerobuf' chunk sizing is\nbounded.\"*\n\n**WHAT I GOT RIGHT AND WHAT I MISSED.**  I argued that repairing `depth == 4' alone would switch the path to\nRGBA and MASK the leak at depth 32.  True for depth 32 -- and I stopped there:\n\n    depth 32   -> RGBA both ways; four source bytes preserved, all four transmitted bytes initialised\n    depth 16   -> STAYS CI: two bytes transmitted, ONE initialised          <- I missed this\n    depth 64   -> STAYS CI: eight bytes transmitted, ONE initialised        <- I missed this entirely\n\n**So the repair masks ONE of three leaking depths and leaves two.**  My sentence said it leaves \"depth 16 and\nthe whole DE path\"; depth 64 was not in it at all.\n\n**And forcing RGBA at depth 16 would not help either:** *\"its PUT would initialize four local bytes but\ntransmit only two.  Its GET would still decode four bytes after reading only two, exposing another helper\ninitialization problem.\"*  The colour type and the transfer width are independent, so no choice of colour\nfixes a width mismatch.\n\n**THE SCOPE RULING IS FINER THAN MINE.**  I said the two defects must be SEQUENCED rather than shared.\ncodex: *\"The defects CAN share a change round, but successful 32-bit MTE behavior would then provide no\nindependent evidence that the shared helper defect was fixed.  For separate rounds, handling the helper\ndefect first avoids that masking.\"*  **So sharing is permitted and costs the independent evidence** -- which\nis a vacuity-floor concern, not a reopening-rule one.  If separated, the HELPER (buf initialisation) goes\nFIRST.  That inverts nothing I had planned but states the cost precisely, which my version did not.\n\n**CONFIRMED SAFE:** the `zerobuf' chunk sizing is bounded -- so the earlier reading of that path stands.\n\n**RE-DERIVED, NOT NEW:** it independently found the RGB GET/PUT layout mismatch ([[sgirergbroundtrip]] --\nnow THREE independent derivations) and the MTE access handler's unreachable width branch ([[sgimtewidth]],\nalready filed, whose title already anticipates that the obvious fix would CREATE a width kill).  **No\nduplicate rows filed** -- the C0 check catching its second duplicate of the session.\n\n*** AND IT SELF-REPORTED A SCOPE VIOLATION, WHICH IS WORTH RECORDING BECAUSE NOTHING WOULD HAVE CAUGHT IT:\n*\"One faulty search exclusion returned forbidden artifact matches, crossing your scope; no finding above\nrelies on them.\"*  The brief forbade reading `regress/artifacts/'; a bad exclusion pattern let some through,\nand the seat said so unprompted and stated the consequence.  That is the honest form, and it is the same\ndiscipline this project asks of its own records. ***\n\n**INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMED 2026-09-07 by the codex seat**, unprompted, on a panel about a different\ndefect in the same file (`#505`, `_scratchpad/panel_20260907_051055`).  Its verdict line ends:\n*\"The cited MTE RGBA branch is unreachable.\"*\n\nThat is a second seat, on a separate brief, reaching this row's central claim from the source rather\nthan from this row -- which is the distinction the seat ledger draws between a reading that RATIFIES a\nbrief-supplied premise and one that RE-DERIVES a fact the brief did not supply.  **The `#505` brief\nmentioned the branch only in a question about whether RGB mode is guest-reachable; it did not state\nthat the branch is dead.**\n\n*And the glm seat, packet-fed, raised a DIFFERENT hazard on the same two lines and was honest about not\nbeing able to settle it: it warned that `src_mode |= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA` yields ABGR (0x30) rather than\nRGBA IF the type field already holds RGB (0x10), and named the settling fact as \"the full mode\nconstruction around that line\".  Checked: `src_mode = (src_tlb << 10) + (((mode & 0x300) >> 8) << 8)`\nsets bits >= 10 and bits 8-9 only, so the type nibble is always 0 and `|=` gives exactly RGBA.  **The\nconditional is sound and its antecedent is false.**  It remains a real latent note -- if that\nconstruction ever gained a type field, `|=` would corrupt rather than replace it -- but it is moot while\nthe enclosing `if (depth == 4)` never executes, which is this row.*\n\n*** PASS-1 DESIGN AUDIT, 2026-09-07 (codex,\n`regress/artifacts/sgimtedepth4dead/codex_pass1_20260907.txt`).  THE DISPOSITION IS **DEFER**, AND\nIT RE-ORDERS THE QUEUE. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"Defer `depth == 32` activation.  Correct the comment now and fix buffer\ninitialization under `sgirebufuninit`.  The one-token repair changes defined fill behaviour as well\nas copy behaviour, and exposes another existing arithmetic defect through MTE.\"*\n\n**So the obvious repair is the WRONG one, for the reason the brief asked about:** making the dead\nbranch live is not a bug fix, it is enabling a path that has never executed in this fork, and it\nchanges **fills as well as copies**.  The addition licence wants a measured delta or a transferred\nwarrant; neither exists, and activation additionally surfaces a further arithmetic defect.\n\n**WHAT TO DO INSTEAD, in the seat's order:**\n  1. **Correct the comment.**  It asserts *\"this makes sure that we copy 32 bits when doing 32-bit\n     transfers\"* -- a behaviour the code does not have.  That is a wrong record in product code and\n     is cheap to fix.\n  2. **Fix the buffer initialisation under [[sgirebufuninit]]**, which is where the defect actually\n     lives.  The dead branch is the MECHANISM of its reachability, not the defect itself.\n\n*** A PREMISE CORRECTION -- MY S2 WAS TOO BROAD, and this is the fourth of the session. ***  I wrote\nthat *every* MTE transfer runs through `horrible_getputpixel` with CI colour.  The seat scoped it:\n**TILED MTE transfers use this helper; LINEAR MTE fills use a separate path.**  So the exposure is\nreal but narrower than the row claimed, and any detector must select a tiled transfer deliberately\nrather than assume it.\n\n**Q1 CONFIRMED with its enumeration, which is worth keeping because it also bounds the selectors:**\nonly TLB selectors A, B and C reach this block -- the source check rejects larger ones -- and the\ndepth field maps as\n\n    field 0 -> depth  8 bits -> bufdepth 1 -> modes 0x000 / 0x400 / 0x800   (A / B / C)\n    field 1 -> depth 16      -> bufdepth 2 -> modes 0x100 / 0x500 / 0x900\n    field 2 -> depth 32      -> bufdepth 4 -> modes 0x200 / 0x600 / 0xa00\n    field 3 -> depth 64      -> bufdepth 8 -> modes 0x300 / 0x700 / 0xb00\n\n-- which confirms `depth` is never 4 and that the intended test was `depth == 32`.\n\n**QUEUE CONSEQUENCE:** `sgirebufuninit` is the round to run, not this one.  This row's own\ndisposition shrinks to a COMMENT CORRECTION plus a disclosure that the branch is dead, and it should\nbe taken as a records rider rather than as a behaviour round.  *That is the option-set discipline\nworking in the conservative direction for once: the answer was \"do less than you proposed\".*\n\n*** INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMED BY THE CODEX SEAT, 2026-09-07, while answering an unrelated design\nbrief on `sgirandomcolour` -- it was not asked about this row. ***\n\n*\"MTE's RGBA-selection branch is dead.  `depth` evaluates to 8, 16, 32, or 64, but the branch tests\n`depth == 4`.  Consequently, these helper calls retain CI, whose PUT fills only one byte even when the\ntransfer is wider.\"*  (`dev_sgi_re.c` depth calculation `:1311`, dead branch `:1365`.)\n\n**And it draws the boundary against [[sgirebufuninit]] in one sentence:** *\"Buffer initialisation\nwould address undefined bytes without restoring the intended multi-byte copying behaviour.\"*  So\nzero-filling `buf` does NOT subsume this row -- the wide copy stays broken, it merely stops being\nundefined.  That is the same boundary the `sgirebufuninit` panel drew from the other side.\n\n*** THE DIAGNOSIS IS A UNITS MISMATCH, AND THE CODE SAYS SO FOUR LINES EARLIER.  2026-09-07. ***\n\n    :1311  int depth = 8 << ((mode & MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK) >> MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT);   /* BITS */\n    :1359  src_x1 /= (depth / 8);                          <- the author converting bits -> bytes\n    :1369  // ... this makes sure that we copy 32 bits when doing 32-bit transfers.\n    :1371  if (depth == 4) {                               <- compares BITS to a BYTE count\n\n**`depth` is in bits and takes 8/16/32/64; the test wants 4 BYTES.**  The comment states the intent\noutright -- *\"copy 32 bits when doing 32-bit transfers\"* -- so the intended predicate is\n`depth == 32`, and `:1359` shows the same function converting between the two units four lines above\nthe mistake.\n\n**AND THE CONSTANTS DEFINE ONLY THREE OF THE FOUR FIELD VALUES:** `crmfbreg.h:268-270` names\n`MTE_DEPTH_8 = 0`, `MTE_DEPTH_16 = 1`, `MTE_DEPTH_32 = 2`.  **Field value 3 -- depth 64 -- is named\nnowhere**, so the two-bit field is wider than the encodings that exist and a fix owes a decision\nabout it.\n\n> **MY ARITHMETIC FOR THE OBVIOUS FIX, offered to the seat as a question rather than a conclusion:**\n> `depth == 32` is field 2, giving `bufdepth = 1 << 2 = 4` bytes AND RGBA, which assigns `buf[0..3]`\n> -- matching the four-byte transfer exactly.  **But that repairs only the 32-bit case.**  Field 1\n> still moves 2 bytes with `buf[0]` assigned, and field 3 moves 8 with `buf[0]` assigned.  So the\n> one-token fix may be the same bug one layer up.\n\n*Design out to codex: `regress/artifacts/sgimtedepth4dead/DESIGN_brief.md`.*\n\n*** CODEX DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07 (`regress/artifacts/sgimtedepth4dead/codex_design_20260907.txt`).\nVERDICT: confirmed dead comparison; `depth == 32` is a justified NARROW correction. ***\n\n**TWO PREMISES OF MINE CORRECTED, both toward less confidence:**\n  * **S3 needed the word PUT.**  The CI *PUT* initialises only `buf[0]`; a successful *GET* reads\n    `bufdepth` bytes into its own buffer and then retains only `buf[0]` in `*color`.  Different\n    mechanisms, and I had written them as one.\n  * **S4 OVERSTATED CAUSATION.**  This branch explains the 32-bit MTE mismatch, but **DE\n    independently reaches the buffer defects** -- so the dead branch is *a* mechanism of\n    [[sgirebufuninit]], not *the* mechanism, and it does not prove every affected copy observably\n    leaks.\n\n*** THE STRUCTURAL LIMIT, and it settles Q1 against the broader repair: THE HELPER CANNOT CARRY\nEIGHT BYTES AT ALL. ***  `*color` is a `uint32_t`, so no implemented type represents a 64-bit\ntransfer regardless of which type is selected:\n\n    8 bits    CI carries the one transferred byte\n    16 bits   CI loses the second; RGBA would make GET consume TWO BYTES BEYOND the two read\n    32 bits   RGBA carries exactly four        <- what `depth == 32' buys\n    64 bits   NO implemented type carries eight; the intermediate uint32_t cannot represent them\n\n**So 'select a type per width' is not a complete solution with this helper.**  A broader repair needs\nexplicit transfer-width handling or refusal of unsupported combinations, and *the scoped source\ncannot establish the hardware's intended 16-bit packing or any 64-bit format*.  The separate\n`DE_MODE_PIXDEPTH_*` definitions do not settle it -- **the helper does not interpret those bits.**\n\n**FIELD VALUE 3 IS GUEST-STORABLE AND REACHES THE TILED PATH** (`:1522` retains the field; the tiled\ndestination cases at `:1341` have no depth validation), and the emulator then treats it as an\neight-byte transfer.  *The separate LINEAR destination path rejects non-8-bit depth by exiting.*\n**But its hardware meaning is unknown**: the missing constant proves only that this header names\nthree encodings, not that the fourth is reserved -- and the regression's `MODE_RGB64` is no evidence\neither, being a DE test value I constructed locally.\n\n> **So a complete MTE repair owes an explicit DISPOSITION for value 3.  Declining it as UNIMPLEMENTED\n> is defensible pending a hardware contract; calling it RESERVED is not established.  A\n> comparison-only correction must leave that obligation recorded** -- which is what this note is.\n\n**Q5 -- THE CLAUSE-(d) MUTANT IS ONE CHARACTER:** after the fix, change `32` to `33` and the branch\nis unreachable again.  `rgb tiled64` and the other nine rows of the RGB script are all expected to\nstay green, **because they exercise DE and never construct an MTE mode.**  *Static prediction; no\nmutant built.*\n\n**AND THE WITNESS HAS AN ORACLE PROBLEM WORTH RECORDING BEFORE ANYONE BUILDS ONE:** MTE is registered\nunconditionally beside DE and dispatches synchronously to guest physical memory, so it is reachable\n-- but *\"an affected copy can appear correct if the PUT's uninitialised tail happens to retain bytes\nfrom the preceding GET.  These are separate automatic objects; that preservation is neither\nguaranteed nor evidence of correctness.\"*  **So a simple copy-equality row cannot be trusted**, and\nfill needs separate validation from copy because fill bypasses the source conversion.  The sibling's\nDE programming also cannot be reused unchanged: its LIN_A destination is exactly what MTE's linear\npath exits on.\n\n*** GROK WITNESS DESIGN 2026-09-07: ACCEPT `depth == 32`, REFUSE MY ORACLE, and it is NOT a\n`detector:` round -- a FILL channel IS deterministic. ***\n\n**MY PROPOSED ORACLE WAS WRONG, and the reason is a fact about the write path:** CI PUT does not\nreplicate `buf[0]` across the span and does not leave a tail.  It writes `buf[0]` at the lowest\naddress and `buf[1..bufdepth-1]` as uninitialised stack, and `memory_rw` memcpys `bufdepth` bytes.\nSo a pre-painted destination is **overwritten with garbage**, not preserved:\n\n    belief                          what the code does\n    CI writes 1 byte, 3 stay paint  CI writes FOUR bytes at MTE_DEPTH_32; the tail is garbage\n    remaining paint IS the defect   on HEAD the tail is not paint -- the row reds because the path\n                                    RAN, which reads as \"the witness never reached MTE\"\n    tail != paint IS the defect     true on HEAD *and* after the fix.  Vacuous.\n    tail equals the previous GET    separate automatic buf[8] objects; neither guaranteed nor\n                                    evidence\n\n*** THE CHANNEL THAT WORKS: TILED **FILL** `dest[0]`.  FILL never GETs. ***  With `bg` chosen to\nhave four distinct bytes (`0x11223344`):\n\n    dest[0]   HEAD (CI)  0x44   -- buf[0] = (uint8_t)bg, the LOW byte\n    dest[0]   fix (RGBA) 0x11   -- RGBA lays [bg>>24, bg>>16, bg>>8, bg], the HIGH byte\n    dest[1..3]                  -- uninitialised on HEAD: **DO NOT ASSERT**\n\n**COPY CANNOT WITNESS THIS AT ALL.**  Source `aa bb cc dd`: CI GET takes `buf[0] == 0xaa` and CI PUT\nwrites `0xaa` at `dest[0]`; RGBA GET reconstructs `0xaabbccdd` and RGBA PUT also writes `0xaa` at\n`dest[0]`.  **The first byte of a copy is the one byte CI already gets right**, and the broken bytes\nare exactly the uninitialised ones that must not be read.  *\"If the witness is a blit, refuse it.\"*\n\n**NOT a `detector:` round.**  The path is reachable and the attempt has not been made; it only looked\nimpossible because the oracle was bad.  Four ways to produce a FALSE \"cannot witness\": MTE LIN_A/B at\ndepth 32 (LINEAR `exit(1)`s -- host death, not a red row); COPY; asserting `dest[1..3]` on HEAD; and\n**TLB_A without bit 15**, which returns silently and looks like \"MTE did nothing\" -- the trap\n`sgi_re_rgb_probe.py` already paid for twice.\n\n**LEAVE FIELD 3 ALONE in this diff.**  A tiled decline is a SECOND SITE -- new predicate, new\nannounce, new accept row -- and it is not the units bug.  The tiled/LINEAR disagreement is\npre-existing and `if (depth == 32)` does not worsen it.  File field 3 and the LINEAR depth kill as\ntheir own row.\n\n**THE REGISTER MAP FOR THE BUILD, derived today so the next step is mechanical rather than a\nre-derivation:**\n\n    CRIME RE base            0xb5000000   (RGB probe: CRIME_RE_TLB_A 0x1000 -> 0xb5001000)\n    CRIME_MTE_MODE  0x3000   MTE_MODE_COPY 0x800 (1=copy, 0=CLEAR/fill -- FILL needs it CLEAR)\n    CRIME_MTE_BG    0x3018   MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK 0x300, MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT 8, MTE_DEPTH_32 = 2\n    CRIME_MTE_DST0  0x3030   MTE_MODE_DST_BUF_MASK 0x1c, MTE_TLB_A = 0\n    CRIME_MTE_DST1  0x3038   -> MTE_MODE for a tiled 32-bit FILL is 0x200\n    CRIME_DE_START  0x0800   ORed into the register address; `if (startFlag) do_mte_transfer()'\n                             at the END of DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_mte), so any MTE write with 0x800 set\n                             fires the transfer -- write DST1|START last\n    TLB_A[0] = 0x8002        BIT 15 IS THE VALID BIT (same constant the RGB probe uses)\n\n*NetBSD's `crmfb_fill_rect` is exactly this path -- FILL, tiled `MTE_TLB_A`, `MTE_DEPTH_32`, X\nbyte-scaled -- so the driver path and the oracle are the same path.*\n\n*** THE PROBE'S MODE VERIFIED SAFE BY READING, 2026-09-07 -- I answered my own review question\nrather than waiting for the seat, per *anything checkable in two minutes gets CHECKED*. ***\n\n`MODE_TILED_FILL = 0x200` passes all three pre-checks in `do_mte_transfer` before the dst switch:\n\n    :1323  dst_y_step != 0 && != 1 && != -1   dst_y_step is 0 (register never written)   SAFE\n                                              -- and its exit(1) is commented out anyway\n    :1328  mode & MTE_MODE_STIPPLE            STIPPLE is **0x400**; 0x200 & 0x400 == 0     SAFE\n    :1336  src > MTE_TLB_C                    src = (0x200 & 0xe0) >> 5 = 0 = MTE_TLB_A    SAFE\n\n**And my worry about the bytemask kill does not apply: `:1413 if (bytemask != 0xffffffff)` is inside\nthe LINEAR arm** (its own debug line at `:1407` says `LINEAR TRANSFER`), which a tiled fill never\nreaches.  *A zero bytemask would kill a LINEAR transfer and is invisible to the tiled path* -- worth\nknowing for anyone who later writes a linear MTE probe.\n\n`MTE_MODE_COPY` is `0x800` and is CLEAR in `0x200`, so this is a FILL and no GET runs -- which is the\nwhole reason the oracle is deterministic.\n\n*** PASS 1 PANEL, 2026-09-07, panel dir `_scratchpad/panel_20260907_170713`.  SEVEN transports\nfired; SIX answered with a verdict.  Seat-by-seat, because a seat that did not answer is a SEAT\nFAILURE and never agreement: ***\n\n    codex GPT-6 Astra max   ANSWERED 127,698 b, full verdict\n    grok 4.6 xhigh          ANSWERED  12,341 b\n    ollama glm-5.3          ANSWERED   7,686 b        ollama deepseek  ANSWERED 4,381 b\n    ollama minimax          ANSWERED   8,735 b        ollama kimi-k3   ANSWERED 8,014 b\n    kimi CLI                *** WALLED -- 403 weekly (7-day) usage limit, 350 b ***\n                            the SEAT IS COVERED by the kimi-k3:cloud substitute above\n    agy 3.7                 DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED -- measurement reserve (owner ruling)\n    Opus 5 / Fable 5        DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED -- held for the 2026-09-09 adjudication\n\n**THE CENTRAL CLAIM SURVIVED ALL SIX**, each by enumerating the four field values rather than\nagreeing: `depth = 8 << (2-bit field)` gives {8,16,32,64}, so `depth == 4` is unreachable, both\n`|= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA` assignments are dead, every tiled transfer takes CI, and at 32 bits CI\nassigns `buf[0]` while `memory_rw` copies four.  **F1 (`depth == 32`) is unanimous.**\n\n*** THREE ERRORS IN MY OWN BRIEF, EACH FOUND BY A SEAT.  Recorded because the premise-audit\nopener is the one piece of prose in this project that demonstrably fires. ***\n\n**1. A WRONG NUMBER, caught INDEPENDENTLY BY TWO SEATS (kimi substitute and codex).**  I wrote\nthat F2 (`depth >= 32`) \"converts a four-byte problem into a four-byte problem at a different\noffset\".  At field value 3 the copy length is 8 and CI assigns ONE byte, so **SEVEN** are\nuninitialised; RGBA would assign four, leaving **FOUR**.  F2 is a REDUCTION, not a neutral\nswap.  *The conclusion (reject F2 for want of a semantic warrant) stands; the arithmetic under\nit did not, and a leak-count argument is exactly where that matters.*\n\n**2. A WRONG RECORD IN THE PROBE HEADER (codex).**  It said \"only the top byte of the\nbig-endian word is read\".  The probe executes `lw` and masks afterwards: it **READS four bytes\nand ASSERTS one**.  Corrected in place -- and the distinction is load-bearing, because the tail\nbecomes assertable the moment the fix lands, which is what `fill bot' now does.\n\n**3. AN IMPRECISE MECHANISM (kimi substitute).**  My `copy top' table said the dest-only cell\ngives 0x00 because \"the write puts the high byte of a low-byte colour first\".  It gives 0x00\nbecause the CI GET *assigns* `*color = buf[0]`, zero-extending; had the GET left `bg` untouched\nan RGBA PUT would have emitted `bg >> 24` = 0x11.  The row is right for a different reason, and\nthe reason is now in the row's own comment so a future seat does not \"correct\" the want to 0x11.\n\n*** TWO HOLES IN MY ROW SET, BOTH MEASURED-CLASS ESCAPES, BOTH CLOSED BEFORE TABLE 1. ***\n\n* **RGB-for-RGBA is invisible to dest[0] forever** (glm and grok, independently).  The RGB PUT\n  arm writes {bg>>24, bg>>16, bg>>8, **0x00**} -- IDENTICAL to RGBA at dest[0].  So a one-token\n  `|= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGB' substitution passed all twelve rows I had proposed.  Closed by\n  **`fill bot'** (dest[3]: 0x44 vs 0x00), and on the copy side by **`copy 4th'**.\n* **Over-reach to depth 16 is unpinned** (glm).  `depth >= 16' fires at 32 (so `fill top' stays\n  green) and is cold at 8 (so `depth8 top' stays green).  **Nothing reds.**  Closed by\n  **`depth16 top'** -- the ADJACENT DOCUMENTED cell, which is why 8 alone was never enough.\n\n**AND A THIRD, FOUND BY THE PRE-TABLE CHECK ITSELF rather than by a seat:** `M8`, a SOURCE-side\nRGB substitution, escaped all twelve rows including `copy top' and `copy 2nd', because RGB's GET\ndrops only the LOW byte.  `copy 4th' (dest[3] = 0x4d) closes it and closes two other cells with\nit.  *That is what the block is for, and it fired on its first mandated application.*\n\n*** A NEW LIVE DEFECT VOLUNTEERED UNDER THE \"if you found something else\" LICENCE (codex), which\nturned out to be ALREADY FILED -- so it is recorded as an independent re-derivation, not a new\nrow. ***  The RGB and RGBA GET arms compute `buf[0] << 24` on a promoted `uint8_t` (ABGR and the default arm shift `buf[3]`; CI shifts nothing -- \"every GET arm\" was FALSE and the pass-2 codex seat caught it), which promotes to signed `int`;\nfor a first byte >= 0x80 the shift exceeds INT_MAX and is UB.  That is [[sgirebufshiftub]].\n**It changed this round's design:** the copy rows use `SRC_WORD = 0x1a2b3c4d`, first byte\ndeliberately below 0x80, so this detector does not stand on undefined behaviour.\n\n**THE SCOPE ANSWER, converged across four seats:** ONE round for the 32-bit cell -- it is the\nonly cell with an intent oracle (the comment names 32) and the only one with a UB-free channel.\nThe 16-bit and encoding-3 cells share a DIFFERENT defect (CI never fills `bufdepth` bytes) which\nbelongs to [[sgirebufuninit]] and must close in ONE round across all widths, **not one round per\ndepth**.  This is explicitly not the `#499` same-class-three-times shape.\n\n> *** THE TRAP GROK NAMED, AND IT GOES IN THE CHANGELOG VERBATIM: F1 fills buf[0..3] at depth 32,\n> so it ACCIDENTALLY MASKS the 32-bit tiled cell of [[sgirebufuninit]].  A later round that rows\n> only depth 32 on this path will look fixed and will not be.  KEEP THAT SIBLING ALIVE ON DEPTH\n> 16.  Do not write prose claiming F1 closed the leak. ***\n\n**SIBLING PARITY -- the one place two seats disagreed, settled by reading rather than by vote.**\ncodex called MTE_TLB_B and _C \"uncovered\" and wanted rows; grok held that the repaired comparison\ndoes not read `dst_tlb`, so a per-label row would pin the helper's ARRAY SELECTION -- a different\nproperty -- and called it \"cheap, not required\".  **grok's reading is adopted and the cell is\nDISCLOSED rather than rowed**, with the escape stated in `PRETABLE.out`: a repair conditioned on\n`dst_tlb == MTE_TLB_A` escapes this detector.\n\n**MEASURED, same script both sides (witness clause (i)):**\n\n    6-row witness   HEAD  5/6    witness_prefix_HEAD.raw\n    13-row detector HEAD  9/12 at 12 rows (re-measured in the clone as M1; see the table)\n    13-row detector FIX  13/13   witness_12row_FIXED.raw + the table's BASE row\n\n\n*** SHIPPED as `2956c7e` on 2026-09-07.  STATE DELIBERATELY LEFT `in work': both stages stand\nat 7/9 awaiting opus5 and fable5, and a row may not CLOSE while a stage awaits a seat.  The work\nshipping and the row finishing are different facts.  Pass-2 receipt in regress/pass2_ledger.tsv;\nsix questions for the adjudication in fable_queue.md 7g. ***",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:30967:   "witness": "harness:four-run controlled experiment, 2026-09-07 -- see codexsubjectrefusal; logs preserved under regress/artifacts/sgirekills/refusals/ and codex_scoping_20260907_CLEAN.txt",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31069:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  **FOUND BY THE glm SEAT** -- a PACKET-FED transport, from the inlined function alone -- in\nan answer I had recorded one sentence from and not re-read (regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/ollama_glm.txt).  Verified here.\n\nglm: *\"`case 4' bounds `tile_nr' above only, so a negative `x' -- possible precisely because the sign check\nis skipped for linear modes -- indexes `re_linear_a[-1]'.\"*\n\n**THE THREE STEPS, EACH READ FROM THE SOURCE:**\n\n    if (!linear && (x < 0 || y < 0 || x >= 2048 || y >= 2048))   <- SKIPPED when linear\n            return;\n    ...\n    case 4: tile_nr = x >> 12;\n            if (tile_nr >= 32)          <- bounds ABOVE only\n                    return;\n            tileptr = 0x80000000 | (d->re_linear_a[tile_nr] << 12);\n\n    x = -1     -> tile_nr = -1   passes the >= 32 test  -> re_linear_a[-1]\n    x = -8192  -> tile_nr = -2   passes                 -> re_linear_a[-2]\n\n*** THE READ VALUE BECOMES A PHYSICAL ADDRESS, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THIS WORSE THAN A STRAY READ. ***\n`tileptr = 0x80000000 | (d->re_linear_a[tile_nr] << 12)' -- so whatever struct field precedes `re_linear_a'\nis shifted left 12 and used as the tile base, and the guest's pixel write then targets `tileptr + ofs'.  An\nout-of-bounds READ of the device struct selects the DESTINATION of a subsequent write.\n\n**THE REACHABILITY PATH IS COMPLETE AND GUEST-DRIVEN, and I traced it rather than assuming one exists:**\n`step_x' is read from the guest register `CRIME_DE_XFER_STEP_X' into an `int32_t'; `src_x += step_x' runs in\nthe XFER loop; and the sanity check at `:734' *announces and CONTINUES* rather than declining -- grok\nindependently noted that same check is the file's only cross-field test and that it does not reconcile.  So a\nguest writing a NEGATIVE step drives `src_x' negative into a linear-mode GET.\n\n**NOT A DUPLICATE of [[sgilinearoob]]**, which is the ABOVE bound (`re_linear_[ab][tlbi+1]' with `tlbi'\ncapped at 31) in a different function.  This is the BELOW bound in `horrible_getputpixel''s case 4.  Same\narray, opposite end, and the existing row's cap does not help here because the guard is a `>=' test that a\nnegative value passes.\n\n**WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED:** which field precedes `re_linear_a' in the struct, and therefore what value the\nOOB read actually yields -- that is layout-dependent and a round owes `offsetof' rather than a guess.  Also\nunestablished is whether any real driver writes a negative step; the path is open, not observed.\n\n*** THE SEAT NOTE WORTH KEEPING: A PACKET-FED SEAT FOUND THIS. ***  It cannot read the repo; it had the\nfunction inlined and nothing else, and it noticed that one guard's precondition excludes the very mode the\nother guard assumes.  The doctrine says packet-fed seats contribute attack REASONING rather than repo fact --\nthis is exactly that, and it is the strongest single finding any seat produced on this brief.\n\n*** ESCALATED THE SAME HOUR: THE OUT-OF-BOUNDS READ LANDS ON GUEST-WRITABLE MEMORY, SO THE TILE POINTER\nIS NOT MERELY STRAY -- IT IS CHOSEN.  Arithmetic and the write path both checked. ***\n\nThe declaration order is `re_tlb_a[256], re_tlb_b[256], re_tlb_c[256]` (uint16_t), then `re_tex[112]`\n(uint16_t), then `re_linear_a[32]` (uint32_t):\n\n    re_tlb_a..c   1536 bytes\n    re_tex         224 bytes   -> ends at offset 1760\n    1760 % 4 == 0              -> uint32_t needs no padding before it\n    re_linear_a[-1] = bytes 1756..1759 = re_tex[110] and re_tex[111]\n\n**And `re_tex` is written by the guest** -- `d->re_tex[tlbi] = data[hwi] * 256 + data[hwi+1];` in the\nCRIME_RE_TEX store arm.\n\n**THE COMPLETE CHAIN, each step read from the source rather than assumed:**\n\n    1. guest stores to CRIME_RE_TEX, filling re_tex[110] and re_tex[111]\n    2. guest sets CRIME_DE_XFER_STEP_X negative (int32_t; the :734 check ANNOUNCES and CONTINUES)\n    3. src_x += step_x drives src_x negative in the XFER loop\n    4. horrible_getputpixel with a LINEAR src_mode: the `x < 0' guard is skipped for linear\n    5. case 4: tile_nr = x >> 12 = -1, and `if (tile_nr >= 32)' does not reject a negative\n    6. tileptr = 0x80000000 | (re_linear_a[-1] << 12)   <- the two guest-written halfwords\n    7. tileptr &= ~0x80000000; memory_rw(..., tileptr + ofs, buf, bufdepth, MEM_WRITE, PHYSICAL)\n\n**So the guest selects the PHYSICAL DESTINATION of a device write.**  The value is shifted left 12, so the\nreachable address space is large; `PHYSICAL' bypasses translation.\n\n*** WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, AND A ROUND OWES EACH BEFORE ANY CLAIM IS MADE OUTSIDE THIS FILE: ***\n* **the layout arithmetic is a DERIVATION, not a measurement.**  It assumes no packing attribute and no\n  compiler-inserted padding.  `offsetof' settles it in one line and MUST be run -- this project has a\n  standing lesson that a struct-layout claim derived by hand was wrong, and the anchor rule's own exemplar\n  turned on exactly this.\n* **no witness.**  Every step is reachable by reading; none has been observed.\n* **[[sgitexindex]] complicates step 1**: TEX writes OVERLAP (the index advances four per eight-byte\n  register), so WHICH values land in `re_tex[110..111]' needs working out -- it affects controllability, not\n  reachability.\n* whether any real driver drives a negative step is unknown; the path is OPEN, not observed.\n\n**RELATION TO THE OTHER ROWS:** [[sgilinearoob]] is the same array's ABOVE bound in a different function and\nis unaffected.  [[sgirebufuninit]] shares the function but is about buffer CONTENTS; this is about the write\nDESTINATION, and a memset fixes nothing here.  **The two must not be folded into one round** -- different\ndefect, different oracle, different severity.\n\n*** THE `offsetof' THE ROW SAID MUST BE RUN HAS BEEN RUN.  ZERO PADDING -- THE ESCALATION HOLDS ON A\nMEASUREMENT RATHER THAN ON MY ARITHMETIC. ***\n\n    offsetof(re_tex)       = 1536\n    re_tex ends at         = 1760\n    offsetof(re_linear_a)  = 1760\n    padding between        = 0 byte(s)\n\nSo `re_linear_a[-1]' is bytes 1756..1759 = `re_tex[110]' and `re_tex[111]', both written by the guest through\nthe CRIME_RE_TEX store arm.  Source preserved at `regress/artifacts/sgilinearnegidx/offcheck.c'.\n\n*** AND THE FIRST ATTEMPT AT THIS MEASUREMENT PRODUCED A CONFIDENT WRONG ANSWER, BY A ROUTE THIS PROJECT HAS\nNOT RECORDED BEFORE. ***  I wrote the test to `/tmp/off.c' from a Bash tool call and compiled it with\n`wsl -- cc -o /tmp/off /tmp/off.c && /tmp/off'.  **git-bash's `/tmp' and WSL's `/tmp' are DIFFERENT\nFILESYSTEMS.**  The heredoc wrote the Windows one; `cc' read WSL's, which held a stale `off.c' from an\nearlier session; the compile SUCCEEDED, so `&&' could not catch it, and the program printed offsets for a\nstruct with `cmd' and `data' fields that appear nowhere in anything I wrote.\n\n**The tell was the FIELD NAMES, not the exit status** -- the same shape as the carrier's stale-binary rule\n(*a binary OLDER than its source*), reached by a different route: here the binary was fresh and the SOURCE\nwas the wrong file.  The existing rules do not cover it: no sentinel was owed, no tree was mutated, and every\nstatus was 0.\n\n**THE FIX IS THE ONE THE CARRIER ALREADY MANDATES FOR SCRIPTS, EXTENDED TO SOURCES: put the file on a\n`/mnt/c' path both sides can see.**  The re-run also `rm's the binary first and refuses if none is produced,\nso a stale artifact cannot answer for a failed build.\n\n*** THE LINK I FLAGGED AS DOUBTFUL IS CONFIRMED, AND THE ANSWER IS SHARPER THAN THE CHAIN I WROTE:\nONE PREDICATE DISABLES ALL THREE PROTECTIONS.  Verified by direct reading 2026-09-07, after the\nverification seat refused the brief. ***\n\nI wrote that I was least sure of step 3 -- whether `src_x' is masked the way the destination `x' is, saying\nthat if it were, the chain dies there.  It is not, and the masking is CONDITIONAL on the very mode that\ndisables the other two guards:\n\n    if (src_is_linear)\n            src_x += step_x;                  <- NO MASK\n    else\n            src_x = (src_x + dx) & 0x7ff;     <- masked\n\n**So `src_is_linear' simultaneously (a) removes the coordinate mask here, (b) skips the `x < 0' guard in\n`horrible_getputpixel' via `if (!linear && (x < 0 ...))', and (c) routes to `case 4', whose `tile_nr' bound\nis `>= 32' only.**  That is not three independent oversights lining up -- it is ONE predicate switching off\nthree protections at once, which is a much better description of the defect than my step list.\n\n**THE REMAINING LINKS, each read at its own line:**\n\n    :715   int32_t step_x = 0;                                  SIGNED\n    :719   step_x = d->de_reg[CRIME_DE_XFER_STEP_X ...];        straight from a guest register\n    :724   src_is_linear = ((src_mode & 0x00001c00) >> 10) > 3; guest-controlled via src_mode\n    :757   uint16_t saved_src_x = src_x;                        so the row STARTS non-negative\n    :912   if (src_is_linear) src_x += step_x;                  unmasked, per above\n\n**MINIMAL REACHABILITY, which the earlier note did not state:** each row restarts at `saved_src_x' (a\n`uint16_t', hence >= 0), the GET uses the CURRENT `src_x', and the increment happens after.  So with\n`saved_src_x = 0' and `step_x = -1', **the SECOND pixel of the rectangle already uses `src_x = -1'** -- a\ntwo-pixel-wide rectangle suffices.  No large geometry, no wraparound, no exotic value.\n\n**STATUS: every link is now verified by reading.**  What remains unestablished is unchanged and stated\nbefore: no witness has been run; [[sgitexindex]]'s overlapping TEX writes affect WHICH values land in\n`re_tex[110..111]'; and no real driver is known to drive a negative step.\n\n*** WITHDRAWN 2026-09-07.  THE FINDING IS FALSE.  `tile_nr' IS UNSIGNED, AND THE DECLARATION IS THREE\nLINES ABOVE THE CODE I QUOTED. ***\n\n    :204   int tile_nr_x = x / tilewidth_in_pixels;\n    :205   int tile_nr_y = y >> 7;\n    :207   unsigned int tile_nr = tile_nr_y * 16 + tile_nr_x;     <- UNSIGNED\n    ...\n    case 4: tile_nr = x >> 12;            <- converts to unsigned on assignment\n            if (tile_nr >= 32) return;    <- 4294967295 >= 32 is TRUE.  IT RETURNS.\n\n    x = -1     -> x >> 12 = -1  -> as unsigned = 4294967295  -> >= 32 -> REJECTED\n    x = -8192  -> x >> 12 = -2  -> as unsigned = 4294967294  -> >= 32 -> REJECTED\n\n**The guard I called `upper bound only' is a COMPLETE bound**, because the conversion folds every negative\nvalue into the rejected range.  There is no out-of-bounds read, no guest-chosen tile pointer, and no write\nprimitive.  Refuted by the codex seat (regress/artifacts/sgilinearnegidx/codex_siblings_20260907.txt).\n\n*** HOW BADLY I GOT THIS WRONG, RECORDED IN FULL BECAUSE THE PROCESS FAILURE IS THE ONLY THING OF VALUE\nLEFT IN THIS ROW. ***\n\n* I filed it HIGH, then ESCALATED it to a guest-chosen physical write address.\n* I wrote *\"every link is now verified by reading\"* -- and had verified the link I DOUBTED (whether `src_x'\n  is masked) while never checking the TYPE of the index variable, which is the most basic property of an\n  index and the one that decides the whole question.\n* I ran an `offsetof' to avoid trusting hand arithmetic on the struct layout.  **That measurement is\n  correct and it was aimed at the wrong link.**  Rigour on one step does not transfer to the step you did\n  not question.\n* **THE DECLARATION WAS ON MY SCREEN.**  I printed lines 184-215 of this function earlier in the same\n  session while checking the mode decode; `unsigned int tile_nr' is at 207, inside that range.  I read past\n  it because I was looking for the mode fields.\n* It is in commit `15633bb`'s MESSAGE as a headline claim, which cannot be edited.  **That message is\n  wrong** and this row is the correction a reader must reach.\n\n**WHAT SURVIVES:** the `offsetof' result (`re_tex` ends at 1760, `re_linear_a` begins at 1760, zero\npadding) is a true measurement, preserved at `regress/artifacts/sgilinearnegidx/offcheck.c'.  It is about a\npath nothing can reach, so it supports no finding.\n\n*** AND THE REFUSAL COST ME THE REFUTATION FOR HOURS, WHICH IS THE SHARPEST ARGUMENT YET ABOUT §C5. ***  I\nasked the seat to attack this chain link by link.  **That brief was REFUSED for being chain-shaped.**  The\nsame seat, asked the same subject WITHOUT the chain -- *which array indexes are bounded on one side only?*\n-- answered in one line: *\"the stated case-4 underflow is absent in this fork: `tile_nr' is unsigned.\"*\n**The refusal did not stop a bad question; it delayed a correct answer**, and in the interval I escalated a\nfalse finding, wrote it into a commit, and reported it as the session's most severe result.\n\n*The lesson is not that seats are unreliable.  I built this chain, I verified the parts I doubted, and I\nwas wrong about the part I never questioned.  The seat that could have caught it earlier was refused for\nthe SHAPE of my question rather than its content.*\n\n**WHAT THE SAME AUDIT DID FIND, and it is not this row's defect:** *\"There are missing checks on complete\nwrite SPANS in TLB_A/B/C and LINEAR_A/B, whose safety depends on caller alignment.\"*  A span check is a\ndifferent question from an index bound -- the index can be in range while the multi-byte write starting\nthere runs past the end.  **Filed separately as [[sgirespanchk]] rather than folded in here**, because a\nwithdrawn row is the worst possible place to hide a live one.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31172:   "state_note": "Derived 2026-09-07 while trying to obey the standing directive *prioritise critical bugs and also\neasy resolvable ones*.  **The directive is unexecutable over half the queue.**\n\n    open + in-work        187\n    carrying `sev`        100   (high 10, med 40, low 50)\n    UNRATED                87   = 47%\n    unrated AND WITNESSED  73\n\n**The 73 is the number that matters.**  A witness makes a row ELIGIBLE under the ladder, so those are\nrows that could be worked today and that nothing has ranked.  Ranking is what the carrier says orders\nthe queue -- *rung is a floor on admissibility, never a priority; SEVERITY and gate blind-spots order\nthe queue* -- so an unranked witnessed row is invisible to the only ordering rule this project has.\n\n*** THE FIX IS NOT A REGEX, AND I NEARLY SHIPPED ONE. ***  A keyword scan over titles buckets 7 rows\nunder host-kill language, 5 under memory-safety and 17 under wrong-data.  **Those buckets are\nCORRELATES.**  What makes a row HIGH here is *a guest can reach it and something bad happens*, and no\nkeyword tests reachability -- which is the deriver rule (*key on what DEFINES the class, not on a\ncorrelate of it*) applied to my own triage script.  The scan is kept only as a READING ORDER.\n\n**Candidates the scan surfaced, recorded as UNVERIFIED reading order and explicitly NOT as gradings:**\n`gdromcondavail` (COND_DATA_AVAIL permanently lies after an overread), `asicindexcase` (a deleted\nguard that was also a bounds check), `gdrompiooddlen` (guards one byte, reads two), `asickillsurvive`\n(four kill rows survive-only, so an OOB read is unobserved), `gdromdmawidth` (a tail store ignoring\n`len`), `sh4pcic15` (an interrupt line that can never be delivered), `asicpermute` (an index\npermutation).  **Each needs its reachability read before any `sev` is written.**\n\n> **DISPOSITION: a RATING pass, evidence-first, one row at a time, with the reachability sentence\n> quoted from the source.**  It is read-only, so it parallelises freely and belongs to the file-reading\n> seats -- the exact work the standing directive says to give codex.  It is NOT a round: nothing is\n> edited, and the deliverable is `sev` plus a one-line warrant per row.\n\n*Related but distinct from [[baretitle]], which counts rows with an EMPTY NOTE.  A row can be richly\ndocumented and still unranked -- most of these 73 are.*\n\n*** MY '73 WITNESSED' FIGURE IS WRONG, AND IT IS THE CORRELATE TRAP FOR THE THIRD TIME TODAY. ***\ncodex: *\"**37 of those 73 have witness strings beginning `none`** -- sometimes followed by\nhistorical measurements, sometimes explicitly denying reachability.  **The snippet counts strings,\nnot demonstrated witnesses.**\"*\n\nMy test was `r.get('witness') and r.get('witness') != 'none'` -- which passes any string that merely\nSTARTS with `none`, and this ledger's convention is to write `none -- <why>` with the reasoning\nattached.  **So the honest figure is about 36 witnessed-and-unrated rows, not 73.**\n\n*The paragraph above this one warns against keying on a correlate, and the snippet printed\nimmediately below it does exactly that.  Re-derive with a prefix test, and note that `none -- ...`\nrows are often the BEST documented ones, since a denial of reachability takes more evidence than an\nassertion of it.*\n\n*** A PATTERN IN MY OWN METHOD, WORTH RECORDING BECAUSE IT IS NOW FOUR FOR FOUR TODAY: EVERY\nCENSUS I RAN KEYED ON ONE SPELLING AND MISSED A VARIANT. ***\n\n    severity          the ledger's field is `sev`              -> 0 of 260 rows matched\n    witness != 'none' the convention is `none -- <why>`        -> 37 of 73 miscounted as witnessed\n    << 24             uint16_t << 16 is the same defect        -> the structural site missed entirely\n    (int)idata        the tree also writes `(int) idata`       -> 226 where the answer is 309\n\n**Each was found by someone else running a different derivation, never by me re-reading mine.**  The\ncarrier's rule says *when two derivers disagree, neither is trustworthy until you look at what each\nMATCHED* -- but all four of these had only ONE deriver, so there was nothing to disagree with, and a\nsingle method produces a confident number with no signal attached.\n\n> **THE PRACTICE THAT FOLLOWS: a census that will be written into a durable record runs TWO\n> derivations with different keys, and prints BOTH.**  Agreement is weak evidence (they can share a\n> blindness -- `sgirekills` measured that) but DISAGREEMENT is a free, immediate signal, and it is\n> the only one available before a seat reads the work.\n\n*** AND THE CLOSURE PASS CAUGHT A FIFTH COUNT ERROR OF MINE, IN THE BRIEF THAT ANNOUNCED THE OTHER\nFOUR. ***\n\nI stated \"note/witness cites a `#NNN` round: **40**\".  codex: *\"I cannot reproduce 40 from the\nstated fields.  `#\\s*\\d+` over top-level `note` and `witness` selects **51**; including `state_note`\nproduces **98 at HEAD / 104 in the working ledger**.  **The field selection needs to accompany that\ncount.**\"*  My KEY2 ran over `witness` plus `state_note[:400]` -- a truncation I never wrote down.\n\n**It also caught that my census MIXED SNAPSHOTS:** HEAD has 184 open/in-work rows, the working\n(uncommitted) ledger has 190.  Both contain the same fourteen commit-bearing candidates, so the\nKEY1 result stands -- but a count taken from a dirty tree and compared against HEAD is two\npopulations wearing one number.\n\n*The two-deriver practice adopted this morning would not have caught either: both are failures to\nstate the DOMAIN, not to vary the key.  **A count owes its field selection and its snapshot, not\njust its command.***\n\n*** ADDENDUM 2026-09-07: THE GAP IS NOT A CLOSED LEGACY BLOCK, SO THE ORDER OF THE FIX MATTERS. ***\n\nRe-derived from the other direction while filing a DUPLICATE of this row ([[ledgersevmissing]], now\nwithdrawn -- see it for the process lesson).  Counting rows that lack the `sev` KEY outright, rather\nthan rows that are unrated:\n\n    total ledger rows                       411\n    lacking a `sev` key entirely            261\n    of those, open or in work                78\n    by state: open 65, in work 13, held 72, closed 93, dropped 18\n\n**The set spans the whole history, not just the early rounds.**  It contains `d1`, `a4`, `n1`..`n6`\nfrom the first rounds -- and also `wdcdataoutdrain`, `gdromlatchcross`, `divratchetband` and others\nopened within the last fortnight.  So rows are STILL being created without the field.\n\n> **CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS ROW'S DISPOSITION: the writer-side check lands BEFORE the rating pass, not\n> after it.**  A backfill alone fixes the past and not the cause, and the queue refills behind it.\n> The check is the `check_cited_artifacts` idiom -- forward-only from a cutoff, so a NEW row without\n> a rating fails and the existing ones warn.  That ordering is the only thing the duplicate\n> contributed, and it is why it was folded here instead of being left as a second row.\n\n*Also observed while sorting: a row with no `sev` is not sorted LAST -- depending on the tool it is\nomitted entirely or it raises KeyError and kills the sort.  Both happened.  That is worse than a\nlow ranking, because nothing reports that the row was skipped.*\n\n*** THREE SUBSTANTIVE codex ANSWERS SAT ON DISK FOR THIS ROW WITH **ZERO** LEDGER ENTRIES, AND ONE\nOF THEM CONTAINED A LIVE DEFECT NOBODY HAD SEEN.  Recorded 2026-09-07. ***\n\nThe three (`codex_shiftub`, `codex_bitsbytes`, `codex_helpers`, all dated 2026-09-07) were verified\nby CONTENT and TAIL -- real answers, no wall text, no echo -- and cited nowhere.  **`codex_bitsbytes`\nnamed the `(1 << width) - 1` idiom in the ARM bit-field handlers.  Its UBFX/SBFX citations were\nSTALE (repaired by `#510` before it ran), but following the finding into the file found the THIRD\nsite, `X(bfi)`, still live with a WIDER undefined domain than either the round had fixed** -- now\nfiled as `armbfiwidth`.\n\n> **A STALE FINDING THAT LEADS TO A LIVE ONE IS NOT A WRONG ANSWER.**  It is the sibling grep the\n> round should have run itself, arriving a day late from a seat.  The carrier's mechanical rule 2\n> -- *when correcting a claim, grep for its siblings before calling it fixed* -- is the rule `#510`\n> missed, and it is recorded there with a prior instance of exactly this shape.\n\n*And the same batch corrected my own arithmetic in a way I then confirmed by brute force: 496\nnonpositive widths of 1024 pairs, not the figure I had supplied.*\n\n*** RE-DERIVED 2026-09-07, AND THE ROW'S OWN NUMBERS WERE WRONG -- WHICH IS THE DEFECT THIS ROW IS\nABOUT, COMMITTED BY THIS ROW. ***\n\n    claimed in the title    measured 2026-09-07\n    47% unrated             34%  (65 of 189 open rows)\n    73 unrated + witnessed  16\n\n**AND MY FIRST RE-MEASUREMENT WAS ALSO WRONG, IN THE WAY I HAD DOCUMENTED AN HOUR EARLIER.**  I\nfiltered `witness not in ('none', '')` and got **59** -- because the field holds prose like\n`none yet -- GUEST-REACHABLE and witnessable` and `n/a -- a harness gap`, which are not equal to\n`none` and are not witnesses either.  **That is the deriver rule's own failure: I keyed on a\ncorrelate (the string differs from a sentinel) instead of on what DEFINES the class (the field\nmatches the enforced schema).**  Keyed on `check_witness.py`'s own regex -- `^(boot:|probe:|\nconstruction:|harness:|detector:)` -- the answer is **16**.\n\n*Three counting methods, three answers (73, 59, 16), and only the one keyed to the enforced schema\nis defensible.  The tell was the same one this project keeps recording: when derivers disagree, look\nat what each MATCHED rather than choosing a number.*\n\n### *** THE CHARACTERISATION IS THE USEFUL PART, AND IT ANSWERS THE PRIORITISATION WORRY. ***\n\nOf the 16 unrated-and-witnessed rows, **14 carry `harness:` witnesses**, one `boot:` and one\n`detector:`.  **The unrated backlog is overwhelmingly TOOLING DEBT.**  So the rating gap is not\nconcealing critical emulator defects -- which is what the row's alarming headline implied and what a\nreader would reasonably fear.\n\n**All 16 were rated on 2026-09-07 by the MAIN LOOP** (2 of them raised on the day's own evidence;\nsee `panelcite0824`).  The remaining 49 unrated open rows carry NO witness of any kind, so they are\ncorrectly ranked below every witnessed row regardless of what severity they would eventually get --\n*a witness makes an item ELIGIBLE; severity orders what is already eligible.*"
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31830:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-08.  **A guard written SPECIFICALLY to stop a panel launching on an empty brief launched\na panel on a brief that did not exist** -- and it is a NEW MECHANISM for the check-that-cannot-fail\nclass, not another instance of the recorded one.\n\n*** THE CASCADE, in order. ***\n\n1.  The diff was written to git-bash's `/tmp/d512.diff` and READ by **Windows** python\n    (`C:/Users/.../Python312/python.exe`), which resolves a DIFFERENT `/tmp`.  *The carrier already\n    records `git-bash /tmp != WSL /tmp`; this is the same trap with a third interpreter.*\n2.  The brief generator raised `FileNotFoundError`, so **no brief was written**.\n3.  *** THE GUARD FAILED OPEN. ***  It read:\n\n        N=$(grep -cE '^@@' \"$BRIEF\")\n        if [ \"$N\" -lt 3 ]; then echo \"REFUSING TO LAUNCH\"; exit 1; fi\n        echo \"ok -- launching\"\n\n    On a MISSING file `grep -c` prints nothing, so `N` is the EMPTY STRING.  `[ \"\" -lt 3 ]` is not\n    false -- it is a **bash syntax error**, `integer expression expected`, which exits NON-ZERO, and\n    an `if` reads a non-zero exit as FALSE.  **So the else-branch ran and printed \"ok -- launching\".**\n4.  `panel.sh` printed `no such brief` and launched **nothing** -- and exited 0, so the background\n    task reported SUCCESS.\n\n*** WHY IT IS WORSE THAN A WASTED RUN: the newest panel dir was still PASS 1's. ***  Had the seat\nfiles been read without checking the directory timestamp, **pass 1's answers would have been graded\nas pass 2** -- a whole review stage discharged by the stage before it.  That is the carrier's own\nwarning about a relative `./panel.sh` launch (\"a SILENT zero-seat failure that looks like a completed\nrun, so you review the previous round's stale seat files by mistake\") reached by a different road.\n\n> *** THE CLASS EXTENSION, and it contradicts something the carrier asserts. ***  The\n> check-that-cannot-fail section concludes that in this project such a check *\"is almost never a\n> badly-written assertion -- it is a claim that some cheap observable stands in for an expensive\n> one\"*, and reports a hunt over 251 gate rows that found ZERO unguarded self-comparisons.\n>\n> **This is a badly-written assertion, and it is in a guard whose entire purpose was to prevent the\n> failure it permitted.**  The mechanism is specific to shell: **a comparison against an EMPTY\n> variable is an ERROR, not a FALSE, and `if` grades an error as false -- so the guard's refusal\n> branch is unreachable in exactly the case it exists for.**\n>\n> The generalisation: **test EXISTENCE before you test a PROPERTY.**  A property test on a\n> non-existent object cannot fail closed, because there is nothing to measure.\n\n**THE FIXED FORM, used on the relaunch:**\n\n    [ -s \"$B\" ] || { echo \"REFUSING: brief missing or empty\"; exit 1; }\n    N=$(grep -cE '^@@' \"$B\" 2>/dev/null)\n    [ \"${N:-0}\" -ge 3 ] || { echo \"REFUSING: only ${N:-0} hunk markers\"; exit 1; }\n    grep -q \"<a token from the diff body>\" \"$B\" || { echo \"REFUSING: diff body absent\"; exit 1; }\n\nThree properties, and each is load-bearing: `-s` catches missing AND empty; `${N:-0}` makes the\ncomparison well-formed whatever grep did; and the CONTENT grep catches a brief that has hunk markers\nbut lost the body.\n\n**NOT MECHANISED, deliberately.**  This guard is written ad hoc at each launch rather than living in\n`panel.sh`, and moving it there is a change to the script every panel runs -- which owes two negative\ncontrols (prove it refuses a missing brief, prove it does NOT refuse a good one).  **That is its own\nharness round.**  Note `panel.sh` ALREADY refuses correctly (`no such brief`); what is missing is\nthat the CALLER believes it launched.\n\n*Provenance: my own guard, written earlier in the same session, in the same shell block, on the\nbelief that it made the launch safe.  It had worked on three previous briefs -- because they\nexisted.*\n\n*** THE FIXED GUARD CAUGHT A REAL DEFECT ON ITS FIRST USE, AND IT WAS THE **CONTENT** CHECK THAT\nFIRED -- not the existence test and not the count.  2026-09-08. ***\n\nBuilding the `sgirekills` round-design brief, I inlined the arm cascade as lines 420-545 and the\nguard refused:\n\n    REFUSING: cascade truncated before LINEAR_B\n\n**The range was wrong.**  `LINEAR_A`'s arm alone runs to line 585 and `LINEAR_B` starts at 588, so\nthe true cascade is 420-660 -- **241 lines, not 126.**  The brief would have said *\"the FULL arm\ncascade, VERBATIM\"* while omitting the sixth of the six windows it was about, and the seat would\nhave answered a question about five arms believing it had seen six.\n\n> **THE COUNT CHECK WOULD HAVE PASSED IT.**  126 inlined lines is a perfectly healthy-looking\n> number; the file existed and was 11 KB.  **Only asking \"is the thing I claim to have inlined\n> actually in here?\" could fail.**\n>\n> That is the denominator rule's own limit, restated: a denominator proves the apparatus SELECTED\n> something, never that it selected the RIGHT something.  A content assertion is the only check that\n> distinguishes them.\n\n**So the three-part guard has now earned each of its parts by a different failure:** `-s` was minted\nby the missing-file cascade that filed this row; `${N:-0}` by the empty-variable comparison; and the\nCONTENT grep by this truncation, which the other two pass cleanly.\n\n*Recorded because the guard was written defensively an hour earlier with no expectation of firing,\nand the thing it caught was mine.*"

